/* True or False minigame statement pool — common myths/misconceptions + surprising truths.
   Each item generated and adversarially fact-checked for accuracy. { q: statement, a: true|false, why: explanation, cat: category }.

   IT IS NOT ONLY HISTORY (Aug 2026, on request: "add more possible questions — they needn't only be about
   history, but any science"). The pool opened with five historical categories and now carries seven more —
   Physics, Chemistry, Astronomy, Biology, Earth science, Mathematics, Medicine — beside the original
   Science & invention. The category is shown to the reader, so it is the subject the statement is ABOUT
   rather than where it came from.
   The bar is the site's own and does not move for a wider subject: nothing here is a claim that "everyone
   knows", every `why` says what the evidence actually is, and a figure that is contested or has been
   revised says so rather than being rounded into certainty.

   PSYCHOLOGY IS THE THIRTEENTH CATEGORY (Aug 2026, on request), and it is the one that most needs the bar
   above rather than a relaxation of it: it is the subject with the largest stock of confidently repeated
   claims that were never established — learning styles, left- and right-brained people, the Mozart
   effect — and equally the one where a famous study is now read very differently from the
   way it was first reported. So a statement here says what was actually measured (Milgram's 26 of 40,
   the 54 per cent of a lie-detection meta-analysis), and where a celebrated experiment has not held up
   the `why` says so plainly rather than repeating the textbook version.
   TWO OF ITS THIRTEEN ARE ABOUT STUDYING ITSELF — the testing effect and the spacing effect — which is
   not a joke at the reader's expense: they are the two best-replicated findings in the whole category
   and they are what this site is built on, so a reader who meets them here is being told where the
   flashcards come from. */

/* ONE STATEMENT PER LINE (Sep 2026). The whole pool was a single line, so every change to it — a spelling,
   a conversion, a new statement — arrived as a one-line diff nobody could read, which is the shape
   `data.js` was given one object per line to avoid. Nothing about the data moved: the array reparses
   entry-for-entry identical to what it was.

   AN ENTRY MAY CARRY `src`, AND THE `why` MAY CARRY MARKERS (Sep 2026, on request: "'True or False'
   minigame explanations should have gloss terms, source citations and metric/imperial uk/us versions").
   `src` is a list of Chicago notes ending in a URL a reader can open, exactly as a card's `sources` is,
   and the `why` points into it with `<sup class="fn" data-fn="N"></sup>` written EMPTY — the digit is
   drawn at render time, so re-ordering the list can never leave a stale number in the sentence. The page
   renders the `why` through `sanitizeHTML` rather than escaping it, so `<i>` for a work's title comes
   with them, and auto-links the glossary through the prose.
   THE THIRD OF THE THREE IS A CONTENT RULE AND NOT A CODE ONE. The units and spelling passes are standing
   observers over the whole document already; what they need is prose written the site's way — METRIC
   FIRST with the imperial in brackets, and BRITISH spelling, the transform being one-way from en-GB. A
   figure with no bracket is shown in metric to an imperial reader and an American spelling is shown to
   everybody. `node .claude/check-truefalse.js` is what measures all of it. */
window.TRUEFALSE = [
{"q":"The Great Wall of China is the only human-made structure visible to the naked eye from the Moon.","a":false,"why":"No individual human-made structure is visible to the naked eye from the Moon, about 384,000 kilometres (239,000 miles) away, and NASA notes that the Wall is difficult or impossible to make out even from Earth orbit without the high-powered lenses used to photograph it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["NASA, “Great Wall,” image article, 2 April 2009, https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The terracotta army guarding the tomb of Qin Shi Huang was discovered by farmers digging a well in 1974, not by archaeologists.","a":true,"why":"In March 1974, local farmers digging a well near Xi'an in Lintong District struck pottery fragments, an accidental find that brought in archaeologists who then excavated the buried army of thousands of life-sized clay soldiers built for Qin Shi Huang's mausoleum.","cat":"Chinese history"},
{"q":"The Great Wall as a single continuous barrier was mostly built by Qin Shi Huang in the 3rd century BCE and the brick wall tourists visit today is that original structure.","a":false,"why":"The brick face and hollow towers most photographs show are Ming work (1368–1644).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Qin frontier walls were rammed earth, their towers solid platforms rather than brick chambers,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and Meng Tian joined and lengthened walls the older states had already built rather than raising one seamless barrier from nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Yousong Cao, Matthew A. Bowker, Manuel Delgado-Baquerizo, and Bo Xiao, “Biocrusts Protect the Great Wall of China from Erosion,” <i>Science Advances</i> 9, no. 49 (2023): eadk5892, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adk5892. [Open access]","Xingyi Li, Yangyang Tong, Xiaoning Ma, Jiachen Chang, and Binyan He, “The Building Concept of Border Defence Facilities of Qin: Watchtowers along the Great Wall in Shaanxi,” <i>Heritage Science</i> 11 (2023): 108, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-023-00951-2. [Open access]","Friedrich Hirth, <i>The Ancient History of China to the End of the Chou Dynasty</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1908), 250, https://archive.org/stream/ancienthistoryof00hirtuoft. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Confucius founded a religion that worshipped him as a god during his lifetime.","a":false,"why":"Confucianism is the body of teaching that grew from Confucius and his followers, and its two central words are <i>ren</i>, the inner moral sentiment, and <i>li</i>, the outward rule of conduct — an ethical and political tradition rather than a religion with a god in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became an official system of thought and behaviour only later, especially after Dong Zhongshu's work under the Han, and Confucius himself said he was a transmitter and not a maker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Bo Hu, Fugui Xing, Miaorong Fan, and Tingshao Zhu, “Research on the Evolution of ‘Ren’ and ‘Li’ in <i>SikuQuanshu</i> Confucian Classics,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 12 (2021): 603344, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.603344. [Open access]","James Legge, prolegomena to <i>Confucian Analects, The Great Learning, and The Doctrine of the Mean</i>, 2nd ed., The Chinese Classics 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1893), 1–2, 14–16, 48–49, 56–90, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Paper was invented in China centuries before it was used in Europe.","a":true,"why":"Hemp-fibre paper is attested in Chinese finds of the 1st century BCE, a century before Cai Lun reported the process to the throne in 105 CE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and the craft reached the Islamic world only after Chinese papermakers were taken prisoner at Samarkand in 751, travelling on to Europe centuries later still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Jean-Pierre Drège, review of <i>Zhongguo zaozhi jishu shigao</i>, by Pan Jixing, <i>Bulletin de l’École française d’Extrême-Orient</i> 71 (1982): 263–66, https://www.persee.fr/doc/befeo_0336-1519_1982_num_71_1_1475. [Open access]","Thomas Francis Carter, <i>The Invention of Printing in China and Its Spread Westward</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1925), 93–98, https://archive.org/stream/inventionofprint00cart_0/inventionofprint00cart_0_djvu.txt. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Gunpowder was written about in China before it was used as a weapon.","a":true,"why":"It was. A Chinese scholar looking back over the record thought the fire trees and silver flowers of Tang festival displays had already contained the mixture, and set down what it does: saltpetre with pine charcoal explodes forward, saltpetre with sulphur explodes sideways, and iron dust with camphor makes it throw sparks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest formulas that survive are in a military encyclopaedia presented to the Song throne in 1044, and they are for an incendiary rather than a propellant — saltpetre, sulphur and charcoal with a long list of resins, oils and poisons, wrapped in five layers of paper and thrown from a catapult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["William Frederick Mayers, “On the Introduction and Use of Gunpowder and Firearms among the Chinese,” <i>Journal of the North-China Branch of the Royal Asiatic Society</i>, n.s., 6 (1871): 73–79, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.533372. [Open access]","Zeng Gongliang and Ding Du, comps., <i>Wujing zongyao</i>, qianji juan 12 (1044), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E6%AD%A6%E7%B6%93%E7%B8%BD%E8%A6%81/%E5%89%8D%E9%9B%86/%E5%8D%B7%E5%8D%81%E4%BA%8C. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Foot-binding was a custom practiced across all of China since the time of Confucius.","a":false,"why":"Foot-binding originated only in the Song dynasty (roughly the 10th–11th century CE), well over a millennium after Confucius (d. 479 BCE), and it was never universal — its prevalence varied by region, ethnicity, era, and social class (e.g., Manchus and many minority and Hakka groups did not practice it).","cat":"Chinese history"},
{"q":"Roman \"vomitoria\" were rooms where banquet guests would vomit so they could keep eating.","a":false,"why":"A vomitorium is a door rather than a room: the name belonged to the doors opening from an amphitheatre's staircases and corridors on to the seating inside, and the Colosseum had sixty-four of them to pour the crowd out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The banqueting story attaches to the word nothing the Romans did with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Philip Smith, “Amphitheatrum,” in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i>, ed. William Smith (London: John Murray, 1875), 82–90, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Amphitheatrum.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Great Pyramid of Giza was built largely by paid Egyptian labourers, not by enslaved Hebrew slaves.","a":true,"why":"Excavations of the workers' settlement and tombs at Giza (by Lehner, Hawass, and others) show the Great Pyramid was built by an organised, provisioned, and compensated Egyptian workforce, not by enslaved Hebrews. The Hebrew-slave attribution has no archaeological or biblical support — even the Exodus narrative does not describe pyramid building.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},
{"q":"Gladiator fights in the Colosseum almost always ended with the loser being killed.","a":false,"why":"Sparing the loser had a name of its own: a vanquished gladiator whose life was granted got his discharge for the day, the <i>missio</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A show advertised <i>sine missione</i>, in which the conquered were never spared, was a different and notorious thing, and Augustus forbade it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["“Gladiatores,” in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i>, ed. William Smith (London: John Murray, 1875), 574–77, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Gladiatores.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Roman concrete, used in structures like the Pantheon's dome, has in many cases proven more durable in seawater than much modern concrete.","a":true,"why":"Roman marine concrete mixed volcanic ash with lime, and seawater percolating through it went on growing aluminous tobermorite and phillipsite in the cement long after it had set, so that harbour structures two thousand years old are still intact where modern Portland-cement concrete in seawater is not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Marie D. Jackson, Sean R. Mulcahy, Heng Chen, Yao Li, Qinfei Li, Piergiulio Cappelletti, and Hans-Rudolf Wenk, “Phillipsite and Al-tobermorite Mineral Cements Produced through Low-Temperature Water-Rock Reactions in Roman Marine Concrete,” <i>American Mineralogist</i> 102, no. 7 (2017): 1435–50, http://www.minsocam.org/msa/ammin/toc/2017/open_access/AM102P1435.pdf. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Cleopatra lived closer in time to the moon landing than to the construction of the Great Pyramid of Giza.","a":true,"why":"The Great Pyramid was raised for Khufu in about 2589–2566 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cleopatra's kingdom ended with the Roman annexation of Egypt in 30 BCE, some twenty-five centuries after it was built<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> — and Apollo 11 put men on the Moon on 20 July 1969, a little under twenty centuries after her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, “Giza Plateau,” Egypt’s Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, accessed 21 August 2026, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/giza-plateau/. [Open access]","Jona Lendering, “Ptolemies,” Livius.org, last modified 2020, https://www.livius.org/articles/dynasty/ptolemies/. [Open access]","“Apollo 11,” NASA, https://www.nasa.gov/mission/apollo-11/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Spartans famously inscribed the words \"Molon labe\" onto their shields as their standard military emblem.","a":false,"why":"Plutarch gives “come and take them” as Leonidas’s spoken reply to Xerxes’s written demand to hand over his arms at Thermopylae, not as anything written on a shield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Plutarch, <i>Sayings of Spartans</i>, “Leonidas son of Anaxandridas” 11 (<i>Moralia</i> 225D), trans. Frank Cole Babbitt, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1931), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Sayings_of_Spartans*/main.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The ancient Olympic Games in Greece were held in honour of Zeus and barred married women from attending as spectators.","a":true,"why":"The games were the athletic festival of the sanctuary of Zeus at Olympia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias says it was a law of Elis to throw from Mount Typaeum any woman caught at the games on the days forbidden to women, and that only one ever was — Callipateira, let go unpunished because her father, her brothers and her son had all won there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Girls were another matter: he says maidens were not debarred from looking on, and that the priestess of Demeter Chamyne watched from a marble altar opposite the umpires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Paul Christesen, review of <i>Games and Sanctuaries in Ancient Greece: Olympia, Delphi, Isthmia, Nemea, Athens</i>, by Panos Valavanis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.20. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.6.7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%205.6.7. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 6.20.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%206.20.9. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Colosseum was originally built and named in honour of the emperor Julius Caesar.","a":false,"why":"Its own name was the Amphitheatrum Flavium, after the Flavian house: it was begun by Vespasian and opened under his son Titus in 80 CE, more than a century after Julius Caesar’s death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name Colosseum is medieval and comes from the colossal statue that stood beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, “Amphitheatrum Flavium,” in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 6–11, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Amphitheatrum_Flavium.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Vikings wore horned helmets into battle.","a":false,"why":"Only one helmet survives from the Viking Age at all — the plain iron cap from the warrior's burial at Gjermundbu, north of Oslo — and it has no horns, while none of the contemporary sources mentions horned headgear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Horns would have been in the way in a fight and worse aboard a crowded warship, and where the art does show them the figures are berserkers, so the museum reads them as display or cult rather than battle dress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["National Museum of Denmark, “Viking helmets,” https://en.natmus.dk/historical-knowledge/denmark/prehistoric-period-until-1050-ad/the-viking-age/weapons/helmets/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Educated people in Columbus's time generally believed the Earth was flat.","a":false,"why":"The sphericity of the Earth was established in classical antiquity and remained standard knowledge among educated Europeans throughout the Middle Ages (taught, for example, in Sacrobosco's widely used De sphaera mundi). The objection to Columbus's plan concerned the size of the globe and the width of the ocean—he underestimated both—not the shape of the Earth.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},
{"q":"The accused in the Salem witch trials were executed by being burned at the stake.","a":false,"why":"The statement is FALSE. Of those executed in the 1692 Salem witch trials, nineteen were hanged and one man, Giles Corey, was pressed to death with stones; no one was burned at the stake, as colonial Massachusetts followed English law, under which burning was not the penalty for witchcraft.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},
{"q":"The Renaissance began in Italy.","a":true,"why":"The Renaissance is conventionally held to have begun in Italy, particularly in Florence and other Italian city-states, during the 14th century before spreading throughout Europe. Wealthy patrons such as the Medici family financed much of its art, scholarship, and architecture.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},
{"q":"Medieval Europeans commonly believed tomatoes and chili peppers were essential cooking ingredients.","a":false,"why":"Tomatoes and chile peppers are American plants. Before Columbus's voyages there were no tomatoes in Italy and no chile peppers in Thailand, and they reached the Old World only afterwards — long after the Middle Ages had ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor did any of it happen quickly: American beans are not certainly shown in European painting until the middle of the 16th century, decades after the first voyages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["“The Columbian Exchange,” Castillo de San Marcos National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/casa/learn/historyculture/columbian-exchange.htm. [Open access]","James R. Myers, Alice K. Formiga and Jules Janick, “Iconography of Beans and Related Legumes Following the Columbian Exchange,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 13 (2022): 851029, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8964180/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Christopher Columbus never set foot on the mainland of what is now the continental United States.","a":true,"why":"Across his four voyages (1492–1504), Columbus reached the Caribbean islands (Bahamas, Hispaniola, Cuba, Puerto Rico, etc.), the South American coast (1498, present-day Venezuela), and the Central American coast (1502), but never the mainland of the present-day continental United States.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},
{"q":"The medieval 'right of the first night,' giving a lord the right to sleep with a vassal's bride, is well documented as a widely practiced custom.","a":false,"why":"Modern historians regard the \"right of the first night\" (droit du seigneur / jus primae noctis) as a myth and literary motif rather than a documented, widely practiced legal custom; there is no solid evidence it was ever an actual exercised right in medieval Europe.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},
{"q":"Universities such as Bologna, Oxford, and Paris were founded during the Middle Ages.","a":true,"why":"The institution’s own name was <i>studium generale</i>, a school attended by scholars from all parts, and by the second half of the 12th century very few places answered that description — Paris, Bologna, Salerno and Oxford were nearly the whole of the list.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What made the title worth having was the <i>jus ubique docendi</i>, a licence to teach anywhere, granted by pope or emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["Hastings Rashdall, <i>The Universities of Europe in the Middle Ages</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 3–5, https://archive.org/details/universitieseur05rashgoog. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Napoleon Bonaparte was unusually short for his time, standing only about five feet two inches tall.","a":false,"why":"Napoleon stood roughly 5 feet 6–7 inches (about 1.68–1.70 m), average or slightly above for a Frenchman of his day; the \"short\" myth comes from confusing the French inch (pouce) with the English inch and from British propaganda caricatures.","cat":"Modern history"},
{"q":"The guillotine was still being used to carry out executions in France within living memory of the moon landings.","a":true,"why":"France's last guillotine execution (Hamida Djandoubi) took place on 10 September 1977 — eight years after the July 1969 Apollo 11 moon landing — and the death penalty was not abolished in France until 1981, so the guillotine was indeed in use well within living memory of the moon landings.","cat":"Modern history"},
{"q":"During the storming of the Bastille in 1789, the revolutionaries freed thousands of political prisoners held in the fortress.","a":false,"why":"When the Bastille was stormed on 14 July 1789 it held only seven prisoners (four forgers, two mental patients, and one aristocrat), none of them notable political prisoners; the assault was largely symbolic and aimed chiefly at seizing the gunpowder and arms stored there.","cat":"Modern history"},
{"q":"World War I formally ended with the armistice that took effect at the eleventh hour of November 11, 1918.","a":false,"why":"The armistice signed at Compiègne stopped the fighting and did no more than that: it ran for thirty days with an option to extend, and either side could denounce it at forty-eight hours’ notice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war was ended in law by the peace treaties, the first of them signed with Germany in the Galerie des Glaces at Versailles on 28 June 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["\"The Special Representative in France House to the Secretary of State, Paris, November 11, 1918,\" document 420 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1918, Supplement 1, The World War, Volume I</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1933), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1918Supp01v01/d420. [Open access]","\"Protocol No. 2, Plenary Session of June 28, 1919,\" document 12 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume III</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1943), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv03/d12. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Adolf Hitler became Germany's leader by winning an outright majority of the popular vote in a free national election.","a":false,"why":"He was not voted into office. The party behind him was the largest in the Reichstag after July 1932 but had never won a majority and lost ground in November, and it was President Paul von Hindenburg who appointed him chancellor on 30 January 1933, at the head of a coalition whose conservatives believed they could contain him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What followed was legal in form: a decree suspending civil rights, an election fought under it, and a law handing the power to legislate to the cabinet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The famous gunfight associated with Tombstone, Arizona did not actually take place inside the O.K. Corral.","a":true,"why":"The October 26, 1881 gunfight took place in a narrow vacant lot on Fremont Street (near Fly's boarding house), not inside the O.K. Corral, whose entrances were several doors away; the \"O.K. Corral\" name became attached to the event later, largely through popular retellings and film.","cat":"Modern history"},
{"q":"Observations of a total solar eclipse in 1919 measured the sun bending starlight by about the amount Einstein’s general relativity predicted.","a":true,"why":"Two expeditions photographed stars close to the sun during the eclipse of 29 May 1919, one at Sobral in Brazil and one on the island of Príncipe. The Sobral plates gave a deflection at the sun’s limb of 1.98 seconds of arc with a probable error of about 0.12, against the 1.75 that Einstein’s theory predicts, and the authors concluded that their results “appear to point quite definitely” to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same paper notes that the theory was already confirmed by the motion of Mercury’s perihelion, so the eclipse was not the first test it had passed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["F. W. Dyson, A. S. Eddington and C. Davidson, “A Determination of the Deflection of Light by the Sun’s Gravitational Field, from Observations Made at the Total Eclipse of May 29, 1919,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London A</i> 220 (1920): 291–333, https://archive.org/details/philtrans06337895. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Thomas Edison invented the first electric light bulb.","a":false,"why":"British inventors had been demonstrating electric light with the arc lamp long before Edison, the first constant electric light was shown in 1835, and scientists worked on the incandescent lamp for the forty years after that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Edison patented his bulb in 1879 and again in 1880 and made it commercially practical, but Joseph Swan patented one in England and Sawyer and Man held a United States patent of their own, so the lamp cannot be credited to a single inventor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["U.S. Department of Energy, “The History of the Light Bulb,” https://www.energy.gov/articles/history-light-bulb. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Albert Einstein failed mathematics as a schoolboy.","a":false,"why":"Einstein was studying mathematics, and the calculus in particular, from about 1891, when he was twelve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He did fail an entrance examination — the one for the ETH in Zürich — and went on to secondary school at Aarau to sit it again, graduating in 1900 as a teacher of mathematics and physics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["J J O’Connor and E F Robertson, “Albert Einstein,” <i>MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive</i>, University of St Andrews, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Einstein/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Isaac Newton served as Master of the Royal Mint and had counterfeiters prosecuted and executed.","a":true,"why":"Isaac Newton was appointed Warden of the Royal Mint in 1696 and became Master of the Mint in 1699; in his role pursuing counterfeiters, he gathered evidence that led to the conviction and execution of forgers, most famously William Chaloner, who was hanged in 1699.","cat":"Science & invention"},
{"q":"Benjamin Franklin proved lightning was electrical by flying a kite that was struck by a lightning bolt.","a":false,"why":"Franklin's 1752 kite experiment detected ambient electrical charge from a storm cloud (sparks drawn from a key on the wet string), not a direct lightning bolt hitting the kite; a direct strike would likely have been fatal, as it was for Georg Richmann in 1753.","cat":"Science & invention"},
{"q":"Nikola Tesla's alternating-current system won the \"War of the Currents\" over Edison's direct-current system as the standard for power distribution.","a":true,"why":"Direct current is not easily converted to higher or lower voltages and alternating current is, through a transformer, which is what Tesla thought would settle it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Westinghouse, holding Tesla's polyphase patents, undercut General Electric to electrify the 1893 Chicago World's Fair and then won the Niagara Falls contract; Buffalo was lit from the falls in 1896, General Electric came over to alternating current too, and alternating current still predominantly carries electricity today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["“The War of the Currents: AC vs. DC Power,” U.S. Department of Energy, https://www.energy.gov/articles/war-currents-ac-vs-dc-power. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Charles Darwin coined the phrase \"survival of the fittest\" in On the Origin of Species.","a":false,"why":"Darwin credits the expression to somebody else in his own text: “the expression often used by Mr. Herbert Spencer, of the Survival of the Fittest, is more accurate, and is sometimes equally convenient.”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The phrase does not appear anywhere in the first edition of 1859 at all, and Darwin took it up only in the later ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["Charles Darwin, <i>The Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection</i>, 6th ed. (1872), ch. 3, Project Gutenberg eBook 2009, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2009/pg2009.txt. [Open access]","Charles Darwin, <i>On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection</i>, 1st ed. (1859), Project Gutenberg eBook 1228, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1228/pg1228.txt. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The mathematician who wrote the first published algorithm intended for a machine, often called the first computer programmer, was a woman.","a":true,"why":"Ada Lovelace's notes on Babbage's Analytical Engine, published in 1843 under the initials A. A. L., describe how the engine could be programmed and give what many consider the first computer program.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Babbage's own account complicates the credit: he had offered to work the Bernoulli-number problem out to save her the trouble, and she sent it back to him having found a grave mistake in his process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Augusta Ada Byron King, Countess of Lovelace,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, August 2002, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Lovelace/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"China was recruiting its officials by written competitive examination for centuries before any European state tried the method.","a":true,"why":"The imperial examination was the written competition through which the Chinese state recruited its officials, and for most of a thousand years it was the main road into government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Song edict of 1044 ordered a college in every prefecture and county, with an examination each autumn after three hundred days of study.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When W. A. P. Martin set the two side by side in 1901 he had to argue the case the other way about: England, France and Prussia had each begun using competitive examinations in some branches of their service, but 'in these countries the experiment is of recent date and of limited application'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["William Alexander Parsons Martin, <i>The Lore of Cathay; or, The Intellect of China</i> (New York: Fleming H. Revell, 1901), 312–14, https://archive.org/details/loreofcathay01mart. [Open access]","Henri Cordier, <i>Histoire générale de la Chine et de ses relations avec les pays étrangers</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Librairie Paul Geuthner, 1920), 100–101, https://archive.org/details/histoiregnra02cord. [Open access]","William Alexander Parsons Martin, <i>The Lore of Cathay; or, The Intellect of China</i> (New York: Fleming H. Revell, 1901), 308–9, https://archive.org/details/loreofcathay01mart. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Wu Zetian, who declared her own Zhou dynasty in 690 CE, was the only woman in Chinese history to rule as emperor in her own right rather than as a regent or consort.","a":true,"why":"Wu Zetian took the title huangdi (emperor) and ruled as sole sovereign from 690 to 705 CE. Other powerful women such as Empress Dowager Cixi wielded power behind the throne, but Wu is the only woman ever to hold the imperial title itself.","cat":"Chinese history"},
{"q":"The earliest datable Chinese account of the magnetic compass is not about navigation at all.","a":true,"why":"It is a bench note. Shen Kuo's notebook of about 1088 is the first description that can be dated and read: he rubs a needle on a lodestone and mounts it four ways — floating on water, balanced on a fingernail or a bowl's rim, and best of all hung in still air from a single filament of waxed silk — finds that it settles a little east of south, which is the first record anywhere of magnetic declination, and adds that nobody can fathom why the stone points at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The needle reaches the sea in the record thirty years later, when Zhu Yu wrote that the pilots of the Guangzhou merchant ships read the stars at night and the sun by day, and in overcast weather looked at the south-pointing needle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Shen Kuo, <i>Mengxi bitan</i>, juan 24 (c. 1088), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%A4%A2%E6%BA%AA%E7%AD%86%E8%AB%87/%E5%8D%B724. [Open access]","Zhu Yu, <i>Pingzhou ketan</i>, juan 2 (1119), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E8%90%8D%E6%B4%B2%E5%8F%AF%E8%AB%87/%E5%8D%B7%E4%BA%8C. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Fortune cookies, served in American Chinese restaurants, actually originated in China and were brought over by early immigrants.","a":false,"why":"Fortune cookies derive from Japanese tsujiura senbei crackers and were popularized in California by Japanese immigrants in the early 20th century. Production shifted to Chinese-American businesses during World War II, which is how the cookie became linked to Chinese restaurants.","cat":"Chinese history"},
{"q":"Measurements above the still-sealed tomb of China's first emperor have found mercury far above the local background, matching an ancient text that describes rivers of mercury inside.","a":true,"why":"They have. Laser radar above the mound reads atmospheric mercury of up to 27 nanograms a cubic metre against a local background of 5 to 10, apparently leaking up through cracks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The text is Sima Qian's, written a century after the burial: with mercury they made the hundred watercourses, the Yangtze, the Yellow River and the wide sea, and machinery kept it flowing, with the signs of heaven above and the whole arrangement of the earth below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Guangyu Zhao, Weixing Zhang, Zheng Duan, Ming Lian, Ningbin Hou et al., “Mercury as a Geophysical Tracer Gas: Emissions from the Emperor Qin Tomb in Xi'an Studied by Laser Radar,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 10414, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-67305-x. [Open access]","Sima Qian, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, trans. Édouard Chavannes, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), 193–94, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The world's first government-issued paper money was printed in China during the Song dynasty, centuries before banknotes appeared in Europe.","a":true,"why":"Sichuan used iron coin so heavy for its value that traders wrote bills against it instead and called them jiaozi; when the sixteen families issuing them could no longer redeem what they had promised, the Song government opened an office at Yizhou to issue the bills itself and forbade anyone else to make them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A note office followed in the capital in 970, and by 998 some 1.7 million strings of cash were circulating in paper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Willem Vissering, <i>On Chinese Currency: Coin and Paper Money</i> (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1877), 167–69, https://archive.org/details/onchinesecurrenc00vissuoft. [Open access]","Thomas Francis Carter, <i>The Invention of Printing in China and Its Spread Westward</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1925), 72–74, https://archive.org/details/carter-invention-printing-china. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Dishes like chop suey and General Tso's chicken that anchor American Chinese menus are traditional recipes brought directly from China.","a":false,"why":"Chop suey is an American invention, and General Tso's chicken was created in Taiwan in 1955 before being heavily sweetened and battered in New York. Neither reflects traditional Chinese home cooking and both were tailored to American tastes.","cat":"Chinese history"},
{"q":"In the early 1400s Admiral Zheng He commanded a Ming fleet whose largest ships and sheer numbers dwarfed anything Christopher Columbus would later sail.","a":true,"why":"Zheng He's expeditions involved fleets of around 200 vessels and roughly 27,000 men, compared with Columbus's three small ships. Even conservative estimates place his largest treasure ships among the biggest wooden vessels ever built, far larger than the Santa Maria.","cat":"Chinese history"},
{"q":"Confucius was a celebrated and influential figure across China during his own lifetime.","a":false,"why":"The offices he first held in Lu asked only that he keep his accounts straight and see that the oxen and sheep on the public fields were fat; he rose to be minister of Crime there and was never chief minister of the state, though it has often been said he was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What made his learning the one learning worth a court's patronage was a Han decision centuries after his death, taken along with the withdrawal of that patronage from every rival school.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Friedrich Hirth, <i>The Ancient History of China to the End of the Chóu Dynasty</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1908), 228–31, https://archive.org/details/ancienthistoryc00hirtgoog. [Open access]","James Legge, prolegomena to <i>Confucian Analects, The Great Learning, and The Doctrine of the Mean</i>, 2nd ed., The Chinese Classics 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1893), 1–2, 14–16, 48–49, 56–90, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg. [Open access]","Yutong Liu, “On the Supremacy of Confucianism and the Periodization of Confucian Classics Learning in the Han Dynasty,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 12, no. 1 (2024): 87–112, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2024.12.1.87-112. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Bi Sheng invented movable-type printing in China around the 1040s, roughly four centuries before Johannes Gutenberg built his press in Europe.","a":true,"why":"In the Qingli period of 1041 to 1049 a commoner named Bi Sheng cut the first movable type anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The account is Shen Kuo's, set down within forty years: characters cut in sticky clay, each as thin as the rim of a coin and fired hard, packed into an iron frame on a paste of pine resin, wax and paper ash.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Thomas Francis Carter, <i>The Invention of Printing in China and Its Spread Westward</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1925), 159–61, https://archive.org/details/carter-invention-printing-china. [Open access]","Shen Kuo, <i>Mengxi bitan</i>, juan 18 (c. 1088), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%A4%A2%E6%BA%AA%E7%AD%86%E8%AB%87/%E5%8D%B718. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"At its height under the Tang dynasty, the Chinese capital Chang'an was the most populous city in the world, holding around a million people.","a":true,"why":"In the mid-8th century Chang'an (modern Xi'an) had roughly one million inhabitants within its walls and was a cosmopolitan hub at the end of the Silk Road. It is generally reckoned the largest and most populous city on Earth during much of the Tang era.","cat":"Chinese history"},
{"q":"Cesarean sections are named after Julius Caesar because he was delivered by one, which is why his mother did not survive the birth.","a":false,"why":"The name goes back to an ancestor rather than to Julius: Pliny says the first of the Caesars was so called from his having been removed by an incision in his mother's womb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> And Caesar's own mother Aurelia was alive and running his household when he was praetor in his late thirties — Plutarch has her keeping watch over his wife, and putting a stop to the rites Clodius broke into.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 7.7, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0137%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Caesar</i> 9–10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library 7 (1919), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caesar*.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Ancient Greek marble statues and temples, which we picture as pure white, were originally painted in bright, vivid colours.","a":true,"why":"Imaging of the Parthenon sculptures in the British Museum found <i>Egyptian blue</i> used extensively, painting elaborate figurative designs onto the carved drapery, together with a purple colourant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sculptors had already worked the marble into textures standing for skin, wool and linen, and the colour went on over those; the study’s own conclusion is that the fifth-century appearance of the building needs reconsidering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Giovanni Verri, Hero Granger-Taylor, Ian Jenkins, Tracey Sweek, Katarzyna Weglowska and William Thomas Wootton, “The goddess’ new clothes: the carving and polychromy of the Parthenon Sculptures,” <i>Antiquity</i> 97, no. 395 (2023): 1173–92, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2023.130. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Cleopatra, the last ruler of Egypt's Ptolemaic dynasty, was descended from Macedonian Greeks rather than being ethnically native Egyptian.","a":true,"why":"Ptolemaic Egypt was governed from Alexandria by the descendants of Ptolemy son of Lagus, from the end of the 4th century BCE until Rome annexed it in 30 BCE, and the last of that line was Cleopatra; government was Greek-speaking and the settler class held the land and the offices, while the villages went on being run in Egyptian by Egyptian scribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Jona Lendering, “Ptolemies,” Livius.org, last modified 2020, https://www.livius.org/articles/dynasty/ptolemies/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The idea that a Roman crowd gave a defeated gladiator 'thumbs down' to signal his death is a well-documented fact from ancient sources.","a":false,"why":"The Latin phrase pollice verso is genuine but its exact gesture and meaning are unknown, and scholars debate whether the thumb went up, down, sideways or was hidden. The familiar thumbs-down image largely comes from Jean-Leon Gerome's 1872 painting, not from Roman evidence.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},
{"q":"Archaeological evidence indicates Roman gladiators ate a largely plant-based, grain-heavy diet and were nicknamed 'barley-eaters.'","a":true,"why":"Bone chemistry from the gladiator cemetery at Ephesus, 22 gladiators among 53 people measured, found wheat and barley the staple of everyone buried there, with nitrogen values low enough that the study puts it down to a great deal of pulse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What set the gladiators apart was strontium: their ratios were significantly higher than their neighbours’, which the authors read as the plant-ash drink the ancient texts describe, and those same texts are where the nickname <i>hordearii</i>, barley eaters, comes from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Sandra Lösch, Negahnaz Moghaddam, Karl Grossschmidt, Daniele U. Risser and Fabian Kanz, “Stable Isotope and Trace Element Studies on Gladiators and Contemporary Romans from Ephesus (Turkey, 2nd and 3rd Ct. AD)—Implications for Differences in Diet,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 10 (2014): e110489, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0110489. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Babylonians understood the relationship later called the Pythagorean theorem more than a thousand years before Pythagoras was born.","a":true,"why":"The tablets are Old Babylonian, of roughly 1900 to 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A round school tablet at Yale carries a square with both diagonals drawn and an accurate sexagesimal approximation of the square root of 2 written along one of them, so its scribe is using the relation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plimpton 322 goes further and shows a method for generating Pythagorean triples, whole-number sides of right triangles, a millennium and a half before Euclid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “Before Pythagoras: The Culture of Old Babylonian Mathematics,” exhibition, New York University, 2010, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/before-pythagoras/index.html. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “Tablet Illustrating Pythagoras’ Theorem and the Square Root of 2,” YBC 7289, Yale Babylonian Collection, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/before-pythagoras/items/ybc-7289/. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “Table of Whole-Number Sides of Pythagorean Triangles,” Plimpton 322, Columbia University, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/before-pythagoras/items/plimpton-322/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Emperor Nero famously played a fiddle while watching Rome burn in 64 CE.","a":false,"why":"Two things are wrong with it. Tacitus gives the singing only as a rumour that went out everywhere — that Nero sang of the destruction of Troy on a private stage while the city burned — and says it is uncertain whether the fire itself was accidental or contrived, since authors have given both accounts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> And the instrument is wrong: Suetonius has him in stage costume on the tower of Maecenas, while Dio describes him performing as a singer to the lyre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 15.38-40, trans. Alfred John Church and William Jackson Brodribb (New York: Random House, 1942), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0078%3Abook%3D15%3Achapter%3D38. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Nero</i> 31, 38, 49, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Nero*.html. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 62.12-18, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/62*.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Great Library of Alexandria was destroyed in a single catastrophic fire that wiped out ancient knowledge overnight.","a":false,"why":"The ancient record describes nothing so tidy. Aulus Gellius says nearly 700,000 volumes were burned during the sack of the city in the first war with Alexandria, and that it was done neither deliberately nor by anyone's order but accidentally, by auxiliary soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ammianus repeats the figure but puts the libraries in the Serapeum rather than the palace quarter, and Plutarch says only that Caesar, forced to use fire against the enemy fleet, watched it spread from the dockyards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The three do not agree about where the books stood or how they came to be lost.","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Aulus Gellius, <i>Attic Nights</i> 7.17, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Gellius/7*.html#17. [Open access]","Ammianus Marcellinus, <i>Res Gestae</i> 22.16.13, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Ammian/22*.html#16. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lives: Caesar</i> 49, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0244:chapter=49. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Under Emperor Titus, Rome's Colosseum was flooded so that mock naval battles with real ships could be staged inside it.","a":true,"why":"Cassius Dio, describing the games with which Titus opened the amphitheatre in 80 CE, records the arena flooded for a sea-fight, alongside cranes made to fight and four elephants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Suetonius’ account of the same hundred days has five thousand wild beasts of every kind shown in a single day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 66.25, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/66*.html#25. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Titus</i> 7, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Titus*.html#7. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Emperor Constantine made Christianity the official state religion of the Roman Empire with his Edict of Milan in 313 CE.","a":false,"why":"The letter Constantine and Licinius issued from Milan, which Lactantius quotes entire, gave Christians liberty to follow their own worship undisturbed — and granted the same liberty to everyone else in the same breath, so it opened the empire to every religion rather than making one of them the religion of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That came later: in 380 an edict of Gratian, Valentinian and Theodosius ordered every people under their rule to hold the faith Peter gave the Romans, and judged everyone else a heretic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Lactantius, <i>Of the Manner in Which the Persecutors Died</i> 48, trans. William Fletcher, in <i>Ante-Nicene Fathers</i>, vol. 7, ed. Alexander Roberts, James Donaldson and A. Cleveland Coxe (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1886), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0705.htm. [Open access]","<i>Codex Theodosianus</i> 16.1.2, edict of Gratian, Valentinian and Theodosius to the people of Constantinople, dated at Thessalonica in the consulship of Gratian V and Theodosius I, Latin Wikisource, https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Codex_Theodosianus/Liber_XVI. [Open access]","<i>Codex Theodosianus</i> 16.1.2, trans. in Henry Bettenson, ed., <i>Documents of the Christian Church</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1943), 31, in Internet Medieval Sourcebook, ed. Paul Halsall, Fordham University, https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/halsall/source/theodcodeXVI.asp. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Mauryan emperor Ashoka renounced war and embraced Buddhism after being horrified by the slaughter he caused in his conquest of Kalinga.","a":true,"why":"Rock Edict 13 is the king's own account of the conquest — 150,000 deported, 100,000 killed and many more dead of other causes — and of the “deep remorse” it left him with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What followed is in the edicts too: officers appointed to work among all religions and for the proper treatment of prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Rock Edict 13, in \"The Edicts of King Asoka,\" an English rendering by Ven. S. Dhammika, Wheel Publication 386/387 (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1993), Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/dhammika/wheel386.html. [Open access]","Rock Edict 5, in \"The Edicts of King Asoka,\" an English rendering by Ven. S. Dhammika, Wheel Publication 386/387 (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1993), Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/dhammika/wheel386.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Silkworm eggs were smuggled into the Byzantine Empire by monks, freeing it from buying raw silk through Persia.","a":true,"why":"Procopius records the eggs reaching Constantinople about 552, brought by monks who said they had spent a long time in Serinda, a country beyond India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the story does not show is a Chinese secret stolen: Serinda is a name the sources use for China, but the raw silk of the day was handled by Sogdian merchants, and the worms were more probably brought from Sogdiana than from China itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Katarzyna Maksymiuk, “‘Industrial espionage’ of Justinian I (527–565),” <i>Historia i Świat</i> 10 (2021): 445–53, https://doi.org/10.34739/his.2021.10.26. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Medieval knights in full plate armour were so weighed down that they had to be hoisted onto their horses with a crane.","a":false,"why":"The weight of plate armour was less felt than that of mail, because plate was distributed over the whole body and limbs where mail hung from the shoulders and waist alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Robert de Vere escaped from Radcot Bridge by swimming the river in full armour, Oliver de la Marche describes a knight leaping clear out of his saddle fully armed in 1446, and Shakespeare's Henry V speaks of vaulting into the saddle with his armour on his back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["Charles ffoulkes, <i>Armour &amp; Weapons</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 64–65, https://archive.org/details/armourweapons00ffou. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Making prisoners 'walk the plank' was a routine execution method among real pirates.","a":false,"why":"Historical records document only a tiny handful of genuine plank-walking incidents; real pirates usually just threw victims overboard, marooned them, or flogged them. The dramatic image was popularized by 19th-century writers such as Robert Louis Stevenson and then by Hollywood.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},
{"q":"The University of Oxford was already teaching students before the Aztec capital of Tenochtitlan was founded.","a":true,"why":"Oxford is one of the handful of places already answering to the name of a university in the second half of the 12th century — Paris, Bologna, Salerno and Oxford were nearly the whole of the list.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tenochtitlan grew on its island in the lakes of the Mexican basin only from about 1375, and the empire it led was consolidated around 1430.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Medieval & early modern","src":["Hastings Rashdall, <i>The Universities of Europe in the Middle Ages</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 3–5, https://archive.org/details/universitieseur05rashgoog. [Open access]","Diego Matadamas-Gomora, Jason Nesbitt, Rodolfo Aguilar Tapia, Leonardo López Luján, Julia Sjödahl, Tatsuya Murakami, and Alejandro Pastrana, “Compositional analysis of obsidian artifacts from the Templo Mayor of Tenochtitlan, capital of the Mexica (Aztec) Empire,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 20 (2025): e2500095122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12107162/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Mongol Empire was the largest contiguous land empire in all of history.","a":true,"why":"At its height in the 13th century the Mongol Empire covered roughly 24 million square kilometres, stretching from Korea and China to Eastern Europe. No other single connected land empire has ever matched that reach.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},
{"q":"The official records of the 1666 Great Fire of London list only a handful of deaths.","a":true,"why":"Contemporary records name only about six victims, even though the fire destroyed some 13,000 houses and left much of the city in ashes. Many historians suspect the true toll was higher, since the intense heat could have cremated bodies and the deaths of the poor often went unrecorded.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},
{"q":"Marie Antoinette responded to news that peasants had no bread by saying 'Let them eat cake.'","a":false,"why":"The line is Rousseau’s. Stuck for bread to go with some wine he had helped himself to, he recalls in the <i>Confessions</i> “the thoughtless saying of a great princess, who, on being informed that the country people had no bread, replied, ‘Then let them eat pastry!’” — <i>brioche</i> in his French — and he names nobody; the scene belongs to his years tutoring the children of the provost of Lyons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Marie Antoinette was born in Vienna on 2 November 1755 and did not reach the French court until her marriage at Versailles on 16 May 1770.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Jean-Jacques Rousseau, <i>The Confessions of Jean Jacques Rousseau</i>, book 6 (London: privately printed for the members of the Aldus Society, 1903), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/3913/3913-h/3913-h.htm. [Open access]","Château de Versailles, “Marie Antoinette, Queen of France 1755–1793,” https://en.chateauversailles.fr/discover/history/great-characters/marie-antoinette. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Napoleon's soldiers shot off the nose of the Great Sphinx with cannon fire during his 1798 Egyptian campaign.","a":false,"why":"The nose had been gone for decades already. Norden's <i>Voyage d’Égypte et de Nubie</i>, published in 1755, after his death, describes coming down from the second pyramid to the Sphinx and admiring its enormous size while feeling an indignation towards whoever had the brutality to maltreat its nose so strangely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is more than forty years before the French landed, so whoever took it, it was not Napoleon's gunners.","cat":"Modern history","src":["Frederic Louis Norden, <i>Voyage d’Égypte et de Nubie</i>, vol. 1 (Copenhagen: De l’imprimerie de la Maison royale des orphelins, 1755), 85, https://archive.org/details/gri_33125008497659. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Catherine the Great of Russia died in a bizarre accident while attempting to have sex with a horse.","a":false,"why":"Catherine suffered a stroke in her palace and died in bed the following day, on 17 November 1796, at age 67. The horse story was invented by her enemies, likely in the French aristocracy, to smear a powerful woman who had seized and expanded an empire.","cat":"Modern history"},
{"q":"Betsy Ross is the woman who is historically documented to have sewn the very first American flag.","a":false,"why":"The National Archives puts it flatly: her involvement in designing and making the first flag is “largely fictitious”, and the story was probably developed in the 1870s by her grandson William J. Canby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her real connection to the flag was as a Philadelphia flag maker who sewed flags for the United States, and the design itself is credited to Francis Hopkinson, a signer of the Declaration of Independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Jessie Kratz, “The American Flag,” Pieces of History, U.S. National Archives, 12 June 2015, https://prologue.blogs.archives.gov/2015/06/12/the-american-flag/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Rosa Parks was the first Black passenger in Montgomery, Alabama, to be arrested for refusing to give up her bus seat.","a":false,"why":"Claudette Colvin, a black teenager, defied the same bus segregation laws and was forcibly removed from a Montgomery bus on 2 March 1955, nine months before Parks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Colvin was one of the four women — with Aurelia Browder, Susie McDonald and Mary Louise Smith — whose case, <i>Browder v. Gayle</i>, was the one that reached the courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["National Park Service, “The Montgomery Bus Boycott,” https://www.nps.gov/articles/montgomery-bus-boycott.htm. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The original Coca-Cola sold in the 1880s and 1890s actually contained cocaine.","a":true,"why":"Coca-Cola's early formula used extract of coca leaf and contained small amounts of cocaine, alongside caffeine from the kola nut. The company began removing the cocaine around 1903 and switched to 'spent' decocainized leaves, becoming essentially cocaine-free by the late 1920s.","cat":"Modern history"},
{"q":"George Washington's doctors drained roughly 40 percent of his blood in his final hours as a medical treatment.","a":true,"why":"When Washington fell gravely ill in December 1799, his physicians used bloodletting, then a standard remedy, removing an estimated 3.5 to 5 pints over several sessions. He died on the evening of 14 December, and modern doctors think the heavy blood loss likely hastened his death.","cat":"Modern history"},
{"q":"In 1932 the Australian army was deployed with machine guns against wild emus and largely failed to defeat them.","a":true,"why":"During the so-called Emu War in Western Australia, soldiers with two machine guns were sent to cull thousands of emus damaging crops, but the birds scattered and proved nearly impossible to hit. After weeks only a few hundred to about a thousand were killed, and the campaign was mocked as a defeat.","cat":"Modern history"},
{"q":"The armoured fighting vehicle called the 'tank' got its name from a wartime cover story that the vehicles were mobile water tanks.","a":true,"why":"To hide the new weapon's purpose during World War I, the British disguised the vehicles as water carriers, and factory workers called them 'tanks' after the steel water tanks they resembled. The name was officially adopted in December 1915 and stuck.","cat":"Modern history"},
{"q":"Thomas Crapper, whose name is linked with toilets, actually invented the flush toilet.","a":false,"why":"Crapper was a real Victorian sanitary engineer who promoted and improved plumbing, but he did not invent the flush toilet. A flushing water closet was built by Sir John Harington in 1596, and the flush mechanism was patented by Alexander Cumming in 1775, long before Crapper's career.","cat":"Science & invention"},
{"q":"The word 'bug' for a technical glitch was coined in 1947 when a real moth was found inside the Harvard Mark II computer.","a":false,"why":"The 1947 moth was real and famously taped into a logbook, but the note itself reads 'first actual case of bug being found,' showing 'bug' was already common slang for a fault. Engineers had used 'bug' that way for decades, and Thomas Edison wrote of 'bugs' in his inventions as early as 1878.","cat":"Science & invention"},
{"q":"Surgeons were using ether to put patients to sleep for operations before they understood the need for antiseptic, germ-free surgery.","a":true,"why":"Ether anesthesia was publicly demonstrated by William Morton at Massachusetts General Hospital in October 1846. Joseph Lister's antiseptic techniques only arrived about two decades later, with his first antiseptic operation in 1865, so for years surgery was painless but still often deadly from infection.","cat":"Science & invention"},
{"q":"Although penicillin was discovered in 1928, it was not turned into a mass-produced, life-saving drug until the 1940s.","a":true,"why":"Fleming noticed in 1928 that a mould contaminating one of his cultures had killed the bacteria around it, but penicillin proved so fragile that attempts to purify it failed and it stayed a bacteriologist's curiosity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Florey and Chain took it up at Oxford from 1938, the first results in sick patients were published in August 1941 with supplies too short to finish some of the treatments, and it was Florey who then interested the United States in making it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["“Award Ceremony Speech,” The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1945, presented by G. Liljestrand, 10 December 1945, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1945/ceremony-speech/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Alexander Graham Bell and a rival inventor filed telephone patent paperwork at the very same patent office on the same day.","a":true,"why":"On 14 February 1876 both Bell and Elisha Gray submitted telephone-related filings to the U.S. Patent Office, with Bell's arriving first that day. Bell was granted the patent weeks later, and the timing fed a long-running dispute over who truly invented the telephone.","cat":"Science & invention"},
{"q":"Nothing with mass can be accelerated to the speed of light in a vacuum.","a":true,"why":"Relativity makes an object's kinetic energy rise without bound as its speed approaches that of light, so an infinite amount of work would be needed to get it there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> However much energy is put into accelerating a mass, its velocity can only approach the speed of light and never reach it: that speed is the ultimate limit for any particle having mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Physics","src":["OpenStax, “5.9 Relativistic Energy,” <i>University Physics Volume 3</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/5-9-relativistic-energy. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A penny dropped from the top of a skyscraper would kill someone on the pavement below.","a":false,"why":"A penny is light and flat, so air resistance caps its fall at roughly 11 metres per second (25 miles per hour) and it tumbles rather than falling point-first. It would sting on impact but carries nowhere near enough energy to break skin, let alone kill.","cat":"Physics"},
{"q":"Glass is a very slow-moving liquid, which is why old cathedral windows are thicker at the bottom.","a":false,"why":"Glass is an amorphous solid, and at room temperature its flow is so slight that a medieval pane would not visibly sag over billions of years. Old windows vary in thickness because crown glass was spun into uneven discs, and glaziers usually set the heavier edge downwards.","cat":"Physics"},
{"q":"Pure water is a poor conductor of electricity.","a":true,"why":"To carry a current a substance needs freely moving charges, and pure water has almost none: only about two molecules in every billion are ionised at 25 °C (77 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What makes tap water, sweat and river water conduct is the dissolved salts, a solution's conductivity rising with the concentration of ions in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["OpenStax, “11.2 Electrolytes,” <i>Chemistry 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-2-electrolytes. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Electric current travels through a wire at close to the speed of light, yet the electrons themselves creep along at less than a millimetre per second.","a":true,"why":"The signal is a rapidly propagating change in the electric field, and it runs along the wire at speeds on the order of 100 million metres a second — a significant fraction of the speed of light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The charges themselves drift at about a ten-thousandth of a metre a second, which is enough because a wire holds so many free electrons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Physics","src":["OpenStax, “9.2 Model of Conduction in Metals,” <i>University Physics Volume 2</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-2-model-of-conduction-in-metals. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Coriolis effect decides which way water spins as it drains from a bath.","a":false,"why":"The Coriolis force is far too weak at the scale of a basin: the shape of the bowl, the position of the drain and any residual swirl from filling completely overwhelm it. It does govern the rotation of large weather systems, which turn anticlockwise in the northern hemisphere and clockwise in the southern.","cat":"Physics"},
{"q":"Lightning never strikes the same place twice.","a":false,"why":"Tall, isolated, conductive structures are struck again and again: the Empire State Building is hit an average of 23 times a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lightning follows the easiest path to ground, and a good path stays a good path.","cat":"Physics","src":["National Weather Service, “Lightning Myths,” National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration, https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-myths. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Sound travels faster through water than through air.","a":true,"why":"Sound moves at a much faster speed in water than it does in air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Temperature and pressure then refract it up and down into what is called the sound channel, in which a signal carries for enormous distances without losing much energy, so a hydrophone at the right depth picks up whale song from many kilometres away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Physics","src":["National Ocean Service, “How far does sound travel in the ocean?” NOAA, last updated 16 June 2024, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/sound.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Absolute zero is −273.15 degrees Celsius.","a":true,"why":"Absolute zero is where a system has the least thermal energy it can have, and it is the bottom of the Kelvin scale: 0 K is −273.15 °C (−459.67 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has never been reached — laboratories have come extraordinarily close but cannot take away the last of the energy.","cat":"Physics","src":["National Institute of Standards and Technology, “Kelvin: Introduction,” SI Redefinition, 14 May 2018, https://www.nist.gov/si-redefinition/kelvin-introduction. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Seen from space, the Sun is white rather than yellow.","a":true,"why":"Sunlight is white: Newton showed it to be a mixture of all the colours of the rainbow, and astronomers speak of the white light of the Sun and stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Air is what tints it — the atmosphere scatters blue more strongly than the rest because blue travels in shorter waves, and the lower the Sun sits the more air its light must cross, so more of the blue is scattered away and the reds and yellows pass straight through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["OpenStax, “5.3 Spectroscopy in Astronomy,” <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/5-3-spectroscopy-in-astronomy. [Open access]","“Why Is the Sky Blue?,” NASA Space Place, https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/blue-sky/en/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Mercury is the closest planet to the Sun, so it is also the hottest.","a":false,"why":"Venus is much hotter, its surface sitting at about 467 °C (872 °F) against Mercury's daytime peak of about 430 °C (800 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Venus's dense atmosphere traps heat in a runaway greenhouse effect, while Mercury, which has no atmosphere to hold the heat, falls to about −180 °C (−290 °F) at night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["NASA, “Venus Facts,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/venus/venus-facts/. [Open access]","NASA, “Mercury Facts,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/mercury/facts/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Astronauts float aboard the International Space Station because there is no gravity at that altitude.","a":false,"why":"Earth's gravitational field about 400 kilometres (250 miles) up is still 88.8 per cent of its strength at the surface, and it is that gravity which keeps the station in orbit at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The station and everything in it are in continuous free fall around the planet, moving sideways fast enough to keep missing it, and it is that shared fall — not an absence of gravity — that produces weightlessness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["NASA, “What is Microgravity?” NASA, 13 February 2009, https://www.nasa.gov/general/what-is-microgravity/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A day on Venus lasts longer than a year on Venus.","a":true,"why":"A day on Venus is 243 Earth days long, which is longer than its year — one trip round the Sun takes 225 Earth days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also spins backwards compared with Earth, so the Sun there rises in the west and sets in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["NASA, “Venus: Facts,” NASA Science, last updated 14 September 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/venus/venus-facts/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Moon keeps the same face towards Earth because it does not rotate.","a":false,"why":"The Moon is tidally locked with Earth, which means it spins on its axis exactly once for each orbit it makes — synchronous rotation, and the reason only one side is ever turned towards us.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a match between two motions rather than an absence of one, which is what the question gets wrong.","cat":"Astronomy","src":["NASA, “Moon Phases,” NASA Science, last updated 3 August 2026, https://science.nasa.gov/moon/lunar-phases-and-eclipses/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Saturn is the only planet in the solar system with rings.","a":false,"why":"Jupiter, Uranus and Neptune all have ring systems: Jupiter's were found by Voyager 1 in 1979, Uranus has two sets of them and Neptune at least five main rings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Saturn's are simply far brighter and wider, where Jupiter's are small dark particles hard to see except when backlit by the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["NASA, “Jupiter Facts,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-facts/. [Open access]","NASA, “Uranus Facts,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/uranus/facts/. [Open access]","NASA, “Neptune Facts,” NASA Science, https://science.nasa.gov/neptune/neptune-facts/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Sunlight takes about eight minutes to reach Earth.","a":true,"why":"NASA puts the mean distance from the earth to the sun at 149.6 million kilometres (92.96 million miles), running from 147.1 million at the near point of the orbit to 152.1 million at the far one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Light covers 299,792 kilometres (186,282 miles) a second, a speed fixed by definition rather than measured, so the crossing takes 499 seconds — 8 minutes 19 seconds on average, and between 8 minutes 11 seconds and 8 minutes 27 seconds over the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["David R. Williams, “Sun Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/sunfact.html. [Open access]","“Speed of Light in Vacuum,” 2022 CODATA recommended values, National Institute of Standards and Technology, https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?c. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The asteroid belt is so densely packed that a spacecraft has to steer carefully to avoid collisions.","a":false,"why":"The belt's volume is very large and the typical spacing between objects down to a kilometre across is several million kilometres, so from inside it there would be far more empty space than asteroids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Galileo, Cassini, Rosetta and New Horizons all had to cross it on their way out, and all of them crossed without a collision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["OpenStax, “13.1 Asteroids,” <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/13-1-asteroids. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Polaris, the North Star, is the brightest star in the night sky.","a":false,"why":"Sirius is the brightest star in the sky, at magnitude −1.5, where Polaris is magnitude 2.0 — taking Polaris for the brightest is a common misconception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What Polaris has instead is its position: it is the pole star, the one that moves least as the northern sky turns each day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["OpenStax, “17.1 The Brightness of Stars,” <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/17-1-the-brightness-of-stars. [Open access]","OpenStax, “2.1 The Sky Above,” <i>Astronomy 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/2-1-the-sky-above. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Human beings have exactly five senses.","a":false,"why":"Aristotle's list — sight, hearing, touch, taste and smell — is still what most people outside the sciences mean by the senses, and it leaves out the vestibular system that gives us balance and the musculotendinous proprioception that tells us where our limbs are in space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also treats touch as a single sensor, where what is now called the somatosensory system is defined far more broadly than that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Thomas Brandt, Marianne Dieterich, and Doreen Huppert, “Human senses and sensors from Aristotle to the present,” <i>Frontiers in Neurology</i> 15 (2024): 1404720, https://doi.org/10.3389/fneur.2024.1404720. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"People use only ten per cent of their brains.","a":false,"why":"The claim is older than the brain imaging usually quoted against it — a BMJ review traces it to 1907 and to self-improvement writing about untapped latent ability, and finds the attribution to Einstein has no recorded source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against it stand studies of brain damage, brain imaging, localisation of function, microstructural analysis and metabolism: damage to almost any area has specific and lasting effects, and no area of the brain is completely silent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Rachel C. Vreeman and Aaron E. Carroll, “Medical myths,” <i>BMJ</i> 335, no. 7633 (2007): 1288–89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2151163/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Goldfish have a memory lasting only about three seconds.","a":false,"why":"Goldfish steering a wheeled tank towards a target gradually adjust their swimming to compensate when the steering is perturbed, and go on over-compensating for a while after the perturbation is taken away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They also do better the second time the same perturbation is introduced — which is memory carried between sessions, not three seconds of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Zhuoxin Liu, Shachar Givon, Ronen Segev, and Opher Donchin, “Whole Body Motor Adaptation in Goldfish Using Fish Operated Vehicle,” <i>European Journal of Neuroscience</i> 62, no. 5 (2025): e70241, https://doi.org/10.1111/ejn.70241. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A banana is botanically a berry, while a strawberry is not.","a":true,"why":"A berry is a fruit that develops from a single ovary with its seeds inside the flesh, which describes bananas, grapes and tomatoes. A strawberry is an accessory fruit: the fleshy part grows from the flower's receptacle, and the true fruits are the pips dotted over its surface.","cat":"Biology"},
{"q":"A human body contains at least as many bacterial cells as human cells.","a":true,"why":"The best current estimate puts a 70-kilogram (150-pound) reference man at about 3.8×10¹³ bacteria against about 3.0×10¹³ human cells — the same order of magnitude — with the gut, and the colon above all, holding most of the bacteria and the whole lot weighing around 0.2 kilograms (7 ounces).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That revised a much-repeated ten-to-one ratio which the authors traced back to a single back-of-the-envelope estimate: 10¹¹ bacteria per gram of gut content multiplied by about a kilogram of alimentary tract.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Ron Sender, Shai Fuchs and Ron Milo, “Revised Estimates for the Number of Human and Bacteria Cells in the Body,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 14, no. 8 (2016): e1002533, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002533. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Blood in the veins is blue until it meets the air.","a":false,"why":"Blood is red wherever it is: bright red where it has just taken up oxygen in the lungs, a duskier red where it has released it in the tissues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That change of shade is haemoglobin, a pigment whose colour depends on how much oxygen it is carrying — so blood that has given its oxygen up, deep in a vein and nowhere near the air, is already a dusky red rather than a blue one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["OpenStax, “18.1 An Overview of Blood,” <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/18-1-an-overview-of-blood. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Different regions of the tongue detect different tastes — sweet at the tip, bitter at the back.","a":false,"why":"The National Institutes of Health name this one outright: “A common misconception is that taste cells that respond to different tastes are found in separate regions of the tongue. In humans, the different types of taste cells are scattered throughout the tongue.”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taste cells carry receptors for at least five basic qualities — sweet, sour, bitter, salty and umami — and the buds holding them sit on the roof of the mouth and along the throat as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["National Institute on Deafness and Other Communication Disorders, “Taste Disorders,” National Institutes of Health, https://www.nidcd.nih.gov/health/taste-disorders. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"An octopus has three hearts and blue blood.","a":true,"why":"The closed circulatory system of octopuses and their relatives comprises three hearts together with contractile veins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they pump is haemolymph heavily enriched with haemocyanin, the blue oxygen-transport pigment that gives the blood its colour, at pressures high for an invertebrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Michael Oellermann, Bernhard Lieb, Hans-O. Pörtner, Jayson M. Semmens, and Felix C. Mark, “Blue Blood on Ice: Modulated Blood Oxygen Transport Facilitates Cold Compensation and Eurythermy in an Antarctic Octopod,” <i>Frontiers in Zoology</i> 12 (2015): 6, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12983-015-0097-x. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Sharks do not get cancer.","a":false,"why":"Tumours have been documented in dozens of shark species, including malignant ones. The claim was popularised in the 1990s to sell shark cartilage supplements, which trials have since found ineffective against cancer.","cat":"Biology"},
{"q":"Bats are blind.","a":false,"why":"The National Park Service answers it as a myth in terms: bats “can see just fine and actually have pretty good eyesight”, and some of the larger fruit-eating bats see three times better than we do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Echolocation is how some of them find obstacles and prey in low light, at dawn and dusk — sound used alongside sight rather than instead of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["National Park Service, “Myth Busters — Bats,” https://www.nps.gov/subjects/bats/myth-busters.htm. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Tardigrades have survived direct exposure to the vacuum of space.","a":true,"why":"Tardigrades endure near-complete desiccation, severe radiation and the vacuum of space, the last of those established by published low-Earth-orbit experiments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They manage it by entering cryptobiosis, a metabolic arrest whose desiccated form is the barrel-shaped tun, which holds its structure for decades and comes back to life in water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Maria Kamilari, Frida Løkkegaard Pust, Andrea Marcantognini, Ask Møbjerg, Ricardo Cardoso Neves, Morten Schiøtt, and Nadja Møbjerg, “At the Edge of Survival: Exploring the Frontiers of Tardigrade Extreme Stress Tolerance,” <i>Molecular Ecology</i> 35, no. 14 (2026): e70471, https://doi.org/10.1111/mec.70471. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Fingernails and hair go on growing for a while after death.","a":false,"why":"Growth of hair and nails needs hormonal regulation the body does not sustain after death, and the forensic anthropologist William Maples called the image “pure moonshine”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is really happening is that the body dries out and the skin round the hair or nail retracts, so the shrunken soft tissue makes what is left look longer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Rachel C. Vreeman and Aaron E. Carroll, “Medical myths,” <i>BMJ</i> 335, no. 7633 (2007): 1288–89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2151163/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Honey can remain edible for thousands of years.","a":true,"why":"The unbound water in honey is too little for microbes to multiply in, and undiluted honey is hypertonic enough to pull the water back out of a bacterium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its acidity and the hydrogen peroxide made in it by the enzyme glucose oxidase work the same way, so very little that would spoil it can grow in it at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Ecem Bolat, Ahmet Alperen Canbolat, Sümeyye Sarıtaş, Mauro Lombardo, Anna Maria Witkowska, Hesham El-Seedi, Mikhael Bechelany, and Sercan Karav, “Honey as a Natural Flavorful Product: A Comprehensive Review of Its Potential Biological Activities and Recent Studies,” <i>Food Science &amp; Nutrition</i> 14, no. 4 (2026): e71645, https://doi.org/10.1002/fsn3.71645. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Diamond and graphite are both made of nothing but carbon.","a":true,"why":"They are allotropes of carbon — different structural forms of one element, each with its own physical properties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diamond is a colourless transparent crystal and the hardest known material, where graphite is black, shiny and soft, and the two differ in density by more than half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["Royal Society of Chemistry, “Carbon,” Periodic Table, https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/6/carbon. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Helium was found on the Sun before it was found on Earth.","a":true,"why":"Pierre Janssen saw an unexplained yellow line in the solar spectrum during the eclipse of 1868, and Norman Lockyer, recording the same line from London, named the new element after <i>helios</i>, the Greek for sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not found on Earth until 1895, by William Ramsay in London and independently by Per Teodor Cleve and Nils Langlet in Uppsala.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["Royal Society of Chemistry, “Helium,” Periodic Table, https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/2/helium. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Adding salt to a pan of water makes it boil sooner.","a":false,"why":"Dissolving anything in water lowers its vapour pressure, so the solution has to reach a higher temperature than pure water before its vapour pressure matches the air above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Salted water therefore boils later rather than sooner, and how much later depends on the number of dissolved particles rather than on what they are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["OpenStax, “11.4 Colligative Properties,” <i>Chemistry 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-4-colligative-properties. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Stainless steel resists rust because chromium in the alloy forms an invisible protective film.","a":true,"why":"A corrosion-resistant, or stainless, steel is at least 11 per cent chromium by mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That chromium reacts with oxygen to form a transparent nanoscopic layer of oxide, which is a barrier to any further oxygen getting in and so keeps the ruddy flaky products of iron oxidation from forming at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["Royal Society of Chemistry, “Chromium,” Periodic Table, https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/24/chromium. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Mount Everest is the tallest mountain on Earth when measured from its base.","a":false,"why":"Everest's summit is the highest point above mean sea level, at 8,848 metres (29,029 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> But Mauna Kea, a Hawaiian volcano that begins deep beneath the Pacific, rises more than 10,210 metres (33,500 feet) from base to peak, which is what makes it the tallest mountain on Earth measured that way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["National Ocean Service, “What is the highest point on Earth as measured from Earth's center?” NOAA, last updated 16 June 2024, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/highestpoint.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Earth is closest to the Sun in July.","a":false,"why":"Earth reaches perihelion, its closest approach to the Sun, on or about 3 January, and aphelion, its furthest, on or about 4 July, a difference in distance of only about 3 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The seasons follow the 23.5-degree tilt of Earth's axis, whose slow variation is what makes the seasonal contrast more or less extreme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["Steve Graham, “Milutin Milankovitch,” NASA Earth Observatory, 24 March 2000, https://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/features/Milankovitch. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A lightning bolt is hotter than the surface of the Sun.","a":true,"why":"The National Weather Service puts the air inside a lightning channel at about 27,800 °C (50,000 °F), which it reckons roughly five times the temperature of the sun’s surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> NASA’s own figure for the photosphere is an effective temperature of 5,772 K, about 5,500 °C (9,930 °F) — and the sun is hotter than that all the way down, 15.7 million K at the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["National Weather Service, “Understanding Lightning Science,” National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration, https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-science-overview. [Open access]","David R. Williams, “Sun Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/sunfact.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Sahara is the largest desert on Earth.","a":false,"why":"A desert is defined by how little precipitation it receives rather than by heat, and by that measure Antarctica is the largest at roughly 14 million square kilometres (5.4 million square miles). The Sahara, at about 9.2 million, is the largest hot desert.","cat":"Earth science"},
{"q":"Diamonds are formed from coal squeezed under pressure.","a":false,"why":"Diamonds crystallise from fluids and melts circulating in the Earth's mantle, rather than from anything buried at the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The deepest of them record melts released from subducting ocean floor 300 to 700 kilometres (190 to 430 miles) down, and thirteen dated from Brazil and Guinea crystallised between 450 and 650 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["Aleksandr Rakipov, Alan B. Woodland, Fabrizio Nestola, Matilde Galiè, Martha G. Pamato et al., “Trace Element Systematics Constrain the Origin of Fluids That Form Gem-Quality Diamonds,” <i>Communications Earth &amp; Environment</i> 7, no. 1 (2026): 469, https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-026-03662-1. [Open access]","Suzette Timmerman, Thomas Stachel, Janne M. Koornneef, Karen V. Smit, Rikke Harlou et al., “Sublithospheric Diamond Ages and the Supercontinent Cycle,” <i>Nature</i> 623, no. 7988 (2023): 752–56, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06662-9. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The shore of the Dead Sea is the lowest exposed land on Earth.","a":true,"why":"Its surface lies more than 430 metres (1,410 feet) below sea level and has been falling as the lake shrinks. Lower points exist only beneath ice or water, such as the bedrock trenches under the Antarctic ice sheet.","cat":"Earth science"},
{"q":"There are more real numbers between 0 and 1 than there are whole numbers altogether.","a":true,"why":"Georg Cantor had proved by December 1873 that the real numbers cannot be set in one-to-one correspondence with the whole numbers, and published it in 1874; the rationals, and even the algebraic numbers, he had already shown to be countable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reals are therefore an infinity of a strictly larger kind than the integers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Georg Ferdinand Ludwig Philipp Cantor,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, October 1998, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Cantor/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The exact value of pi has been calculated in full.","a":false,"why":"Lambert proved in 1761 that π is irrational, so its decimal expansion neither ends nor repeats and no finite calculation can complete it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lindemann went further in 1882 and proved it transcendental — not the root of any algebraic equation with rational coefficients.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Carl Louis Ferdinand von Lindemann,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Lindemann/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"In a room of 23 people, the odds are better than even that two of them share a birthday.","a":true,"why":"The comparison is not between one person and the rest but between all 253 possible pairs, which makes a match far likelier than intuition suggests. With 23 people the probability is about 50.7 per cent, and with 70 it is over 99.","cat":"Mathematics"},
{"q":"Cracking your knuckles causes arthritis.","a":false,"why":"Real-time magnetic resonance imaging of ten finger joints pulled apart shows the sound comes from a cavity forming in the joint fluid at the moment the surfaces separate, rather than from a bubble collapsing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A case-control study of 215 people aged 50 to 89 found hand osteoarthritis about as common among habitual knuckle-crackers as among everyone else — 18.1 per cent against 21.5 — with no association at any joint however long or often they had done it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Gregory N. Kawchuk, Jerome Fryer, Jacob L. Jaremko, Hongbo Zeng, Lindsay Rowe, and Richard Thompson, “Real-Time Visualization of Joint Cavitation,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 4 (2015): e0119470, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0119470. [Open access]","Kevin deWeber, Mariusz Olszewski, and Rebecca Ortolano, “Knuckle Cracking and Hand Osteoarthritis,” <i>Journal of the American Board of Family Medicine</i> 24, no. 2 (2011): 169–74, https://doi.org/10.3122/jabfm.2011.02.100156. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Most body heat is lost through the head.","a":false,"why":"Heat leaves roughly in proportion to the area of skin exposed, and the head is about a tenth of the body's surface. The idea came from a military experiment in which subjects were dressed in survival suits with only their heads uncovered.","cat":"Medicine"},
{"q":"Antibiotics do nothing against the viruses that cause colds and flu.","a":true,"why":"Most colds and flu are caused by viruses, and antibiotics are not effective against them; the drugs that work on viruses are the antivirals, used for influenza, HIV and herpes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taking an antibiotic for a viral infection cannot shorten it, and it adds to the resistance that makes the drug less useful when a bacterial infection genuinely needs it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control, “Factsheet for the general public — Antimicrobial resistance,” https://www.ecdc.europa.eu/en/antimicrobial-resistance/facts/factsheets/general-public. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Taking vitamin C stops you catching a cold.","a":false,"why":"A Cochrane review pooling 29 trial comparisons over 11,306 people found that regular supplementation did not reduce how often people in the general community caught colds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same review found colds shortened by 8 per cent in adults and 14 per cent in children, and one narrow group whose risk of catching one was roughly halved: 598 marathon runners, skiers and soldiers on subarctic exercises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Harri Hemilä and Elizabeth Chalker, “Vitamin C for Preventing and Treating the Common Cold,” <i>Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews</i> 2013, no. 1 (2013): CD000980, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8078152/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The appendix is a useless leftover with no known function.","a":false,"why":"The vermiform appendix was long considered vestigial, and accumulating evidence now puts it in the gut-associated lymphoid tissue and in the regulation of the gut's own microbes, with a microbial population of its own rather than a passive extension of what passes through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One influential proposal makes it a protected reservoir of commensal biofilms that can help repopulate the colon after a diarrhoeal or infectious upset — a hypothesis rather than a settled fact, but a long way from no known function.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Shuai Wang, Jingbo Lv, Li Wang, and Xueyuan Cao, “Appendectomy and Colorectal Cancer: A Mini Review from the Perspective of Gut Microbiota and Mucosal Immunity,” <i>Frontiers in Cellular and Infection Microbiology</i> 16 (2026): 1859485, https://doi.org/10.3389/fcimb.2026.1859485. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A microwave oven cooks food from the inside out.","a":false,"why":"Microwaves penetrate only a couple of centimetres before their energy is absorbed, so they heat the outer layer and the middle warms by conduction, much as in a conventional oven. Food can seem to cook from within because the dry surface loses heat to the air while the moist interior does not.","cat":"Science & invention"},
{"q":"The first published computer program was written by a woman.","a":true,"why":"The notes appended to Menabrea's memoir in <i>Scientific Memoirs</i> for 1843, signed only A. A. L., end by following in detail the steps through which the engine could compute the numbers of Bernoulli, with a diagram and table worked out for one of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their author was Ada Lovelace, and what they set out is what many consider the first computer program.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Augusta Ada Byron King, Countess of Lovelace,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, August 2002, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Lovelace/. [Open access]","Ada Augusta, Countess of Lovelace, “Notes by the Translator, Note G,” appended to L. F. Menabrea, “Sketch of the Analytical Engine Invented by Charles Babbage, Esq.,” in <i>Scientific Memoirs</i>, ed. Richard Taylor, vol. 3 (London: Richard and John E. Taylor, 1843), https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.53369. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"People are either left-brained and logical or right-brained and creative.","a":false,"why":"Some functions are lateralised — the left-lateralised hubs include Broca’s area and Wernicke’s area — but a 2013 study that measured lateralisation between 7,266 regions in 1,011 people found the pattern to be local rather than whole-brain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its data were not consistent with individuals having a generally ‘left-brained’ or ‘right-brained’ network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["Jared A. Nielsen, Brandon A. Zielinski, Michael A. Ferguson, Janet E. Lainhart, and Jeffrey S. Anderson, “An Evaluation of the Left-Brain vs. Right-Brain Hypothesis with Resting State Functional Connectivity Magnetic Resonance Imaging,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71275, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071275. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Listening to Mozart has been shown to make children more intelligent.","a":false,"why":"The effect began with a 1993 report of better performance on a spatial task after listening to one Mozart sonata, a finding a 2023 meta-analysis puts down to low study power and bias-related measurement artefacts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The claims about lasting gains in children’s intelligence were added afterwards by others, and are what carried the idea into public belief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["Sandra Oberleiter and Jakob Pietschnig, “Unfounded Authority, Underpowered Studies, and Non-Transparent Reporting Perpetuate the Mozart Effect Myth: A Multiverse Meta-Analysis,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 3175, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-30206-w. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"In Stanley Milgram's obedience experiments most volunteers went on delivering what they believed were dangerous electric shocks when an experimenter told them to.","a":true,"why":"Milgram recruited forty men and had them shock a learner in 15-volt steps up to 450 volts, with both the shocks and the learner's answers staged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the experimenter told them to carry on, 65 per cent went to the maximum setting — and to the point at which the learner had stopped responding altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["OpenStax, “12.4 Conformity, Compliance, and Obedience,” <i>Psychology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/12-4-conformity-compliance-and-obedience. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Once a memory has been stored, recalling it leaves it unchanged, like replaying a recording.","a":false,"why":"Remembering is reconstruction rather than playback: bringing an old memory up combines it with newer ones, so events can be added and what we think we remember can change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elizabeth Loftus's misinformation work is the standing demonstration — shown inaccurate information after an event, a person may misremember the event itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["OpenStax, “8.3 Problems with Memory,” <i>Psychology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/8-3-problems-with-memory. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"People can reliably spot a liar from body language at well above chance.","a":false,"why":"A meta-analysis of some two hundred studies put average accuracy at about 54 per cent, barely better than a coin toss, and found police officers and other supposed experts no better than students. The confident cues people rely on, such as looking away, are not associated with lying.","cat":"Psychology"},
{"q":"The murder that gave psychology the 'bystander effect' was reported with details that later turned out to be substantially wrong.","a":true,"why":"The 1964 killing of Kitty Genovese in New York was reported as having been watched by 38 witnesses who did nothing, a figure the newspaper account cannot support: several people did not see the attack, at least two called the police, and a neighbour went to her. The effect itself has since been demonstrated in controlled experiments.","cat":"Psychology"},
{"q":"Opposites attract: people tend to pair up with partners unlike themselves.","a":false,"why":"The consistent finding is the reverse. Assortative mating — the tendency to choose a partner similar to oneself — is described as a ubiquitous pattern in mate choice, well documented in humans across physical, personality and demographic characteristics, and in other species besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["Kaitlyn T. Harper and Brendan P. Zietsch, “Assortative Mating Is a Natural Consequence of Heritable Variation in Preferences and Preferred Traits,” <i>Psychological Science</i> 36, no. 10 (2025): 771–79, https://doi.org/10.1177/09567976251365900. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Teaching pupils in their own 'learning style' — visual, auditory or kinaesthetic — measurably improves how much they learn.","a":false,"why":"People readily report a preferred style and the belief is common in teaching: 58 per cent of 114 UK university academics surveyed in 2017 said they believed in using learning styles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the experiments test is whether matching instruction to that style helps, and a review restricted to studies that screened learners for a preference, taught them in matched and unmatched conditions and looked for a crossover interaction found the effects very low and not significant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["Philip M. Newton and Mahallad Miah, “Evidence-Based Higher Education — Is the Learning Styles ‘Myth’ Important?,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 8 (2017): 444, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2017.00444. [Open access]","Karoline Aslaksen and Håvard Lorås, “The Modality-Specific Learning Style Hypothesis: A Mini-Review,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 9 (2018): 1538, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2018.01538. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Testing yourself on material produces better long-term retention than spending the same time re-reading it.","a":true,"why":"A doctoral student at Illinois showed practice tests improving performance in 1909, and “more than 100 years of research has revealed that taking practice tests (versus merely rereading the material to be learned) can substantially boost student learning”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The review that rated ten study strategies put practice testing among the two most effective of them and rereading among the least, despite rereading being what most students do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["John Dunlosky, “Strengthening the Student Toolbox: Study Strategies to Boost Learning,” <i>American Educator</i> 37, no. 3 (Fall 2013), https://www.aft.org/ae/fall2013/dunlosky. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Spreading study sessions out over days produces better long-term recall than putting the same total hours into one sitting.","a":true,"why":"Students “will retain knowledge and skills for a longer period of time when they distribute their practice than when they mass it, even if they use the same amount of time massing and distributing their practice”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many students believe the opposite, because massed study makes the material feel familiar while it is in front of them — which is part of why cramming keeps being chosen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["John Dunlosky, “Strengthening the Student Toolbox: Study Strategies to Boost Learning,” <i>American Educator</i> 37, no. 3 (Fall 2013), https://www.aft.org/ae/fall2013/dunlosky. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Stanford prison experiment is trusted because its results have been reproduced by other researchers.","a":false,"why":"It has not been successfully replicated, and recordings from the study itself show the guards were coached towards harshness rather than falling into it. It ran without a control condition on a dozen guards for six days, and is now taught as much for its methodological problems as for its conclusions.","cat":"Psychology"},
{"q":"A famous 1957 experiment proved that split-second messages spliced into a cinema film made the audience buy more popcorn.","a":false,"why":"The market researcher James Vicary announced the result and never produced the data; in 1962 he conceded that the study was a stunt and the figures made up. Attempts to reproduce such an effect have found nothing of the kind, but the claim entered public belief and has never fully left it.","cat":"Psychology"},
{"q":"A placebo can produce a measurable improvement even when the patient is told outright that it is a placebo.","a":true,"why":"In a 2010 trial 80 patients with irritable bowel syndrome were randomised either to pills openly described as inert or to no treatment with the same contact with staff, and the placebo group reported significantly greater improvement at both 11 days and three weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every outcome measured was one the patients reported about themselves rather than a measure of the disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Psychology","src":["Ted J. Kaptchuk, Elizabeth Friedlander, John M. Kelley, M. Norma Sanchez, Efi Kokkotou, Joyce P. Singer, Magda Kowalczykowski, Franklin G. Miller, Irving Kirsch, and Anthony J. Lembo, “Placebos without Deception: A Randomized Controlled Trial in Irritable Bowel Syndrome,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 5, no. 12 (2010): e15591, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0015591. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Every even number greater than two has been proved to be the sum of two prime numbers.","a":false,"why":"This is Goldbach's conjecture, made in 1742 in a letter to Euler, and it is still an open question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Computers have checked it for every even number up to at least 400 trillion, which is evidence and not a proof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Christian Goldbach,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, August 2006, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Goldbach/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"There is no largest prime number, and this has been known since antiquity.","a":true,"why":"Euclid gives the proof in the <i>Elements</i>, as the proposition that prime numbers are more than any assigned multitude of prime numbers: take the least number the listed primes all measure, add one, and what results is divisible by none of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The argument is about two and a half thousand years old and has never needed improving.","cat":"Mathematics","src":["Euclid, <i>Elements</i> 9.20, trans. Thomas L. Heath (New York: Dover, 1956), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0086:book=9:type=Prop:number=20. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Fermat's Last Theorem went unproved for more than three centuries after it was written down.","a":true,"why":"Fermat wrote it in the margin of his Diophantus — “I have discovered a truly remarkable proof which this margin is too small to contain” — and the note only became known when his son Samuel published the edition carrying it in 1670.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Andrew Wiles announced a proof in June 1993, withdrew it when a hole was found, and worked with Richard Taylor through 1994 before the argument held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Pierre de Fermat,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Fermat/. [Open access]","J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Fermat's last theorem,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Fermat%27s_last_theorem/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A Möbius strip has only one side and one edge.","a":true,"why":"Join the two ends of a paper strip with a half twist, and a path traced from any point on the surface runs through the point that appears to lie on the other side of it, so there is only one side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Möbius came upon the surface in 1858, although by both publication and discovery the precedence belongs to Johann Listing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “August Ferdinand Möbius,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, January 1997, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Mobius/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Zero was used as a number in Europe well before it reached India.","a":false,"why":"It went the other way: Brahmagupta set out rules for arithmetic with zero and with negative numbers in seventh-century India, the first attempt anyone is known to have made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zero reached Europe through Arabic and Islamic mathematics, and when Fibonacci described the nine Indian symbols and the sign 0 around 1200 it went on not being widely used for a long time after that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “A history of Zero,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, November 2000, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Zero/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"It has been proved that a circle cannot be squared with compass and straightedge.","a":true,"why":"Squaring the circle — constructing a square of the same area with ruler and compasses alone — was one of the classical problems of Greek mathematics, and Lambert's 1761 proof that π is irrational was not enough to settle it, since some algebraic numbers can be constructed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lindemann's 1882 proof that π is transcendental “finally established that squaring the circle with ruler and compasses is insoluble”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Mathematics","src":["J. J. O'Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Carl Louis Ferdinand von Lindemann,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Lindemann/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The chemical symbols for iron, lead and gold are taken from their Latin names rather than their English ones.","a":true,"why":"Fe, Pb and Au come from <i>ferrum</i>, <i>plumbum</i> and <i>aurum</i>, where the English names are Anglo-Saxon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Several other symbols work the same way, among them Ag for <i>argentum</i> and Sn for <i>stannum</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["“Iron,” Periodic Table of Elements, Los Alamos National Laboratory, https://periodic.lanl.gov/26.shtml. [Open access]","Royal Society of Chemistry, “Lead,” Periodic Table, https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/82/lead. [Open access]","Royal Society of Chemistry, “Gold,” Periodic Table, https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/79/gold. [Open access]","“Silver,” Periodic Table of Elements, Los Alamos National Laboratory, https://periodic.lanl.gov/47.shtml. [Open access]","“Tin,” Periodic Table of Elements, Los Alamos National Laboratory, https://periodic.lanl.gov/50.shtml. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Water can be made to boil at room temperature.","a":true,"why":"A liquid boils where its vapour pressure matches the pressure around it, and the liquid–vapour curve gives water's boiling point at any pressure — 100 °C (212 °F) at one atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seal water at 25 °C (77 °F) and pump the air out, and the vapour above it settles at 0.03 atmospheres, which is the pressure at which room-temperature water boils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["OpenStax, “10.4 Phase Diagrams,” <i>Chemistry 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/10-4-phase-diagrams. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Iron that has rusted weighs less than the iron it formed from.","a":false,"why":"It weighs more. Lavoisier burnt iron in a closed vessel and found that the weight the metal gained was exactly the weight the air inside lost, so rust is iron that has taken something on rather than iron that has given something up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The red oxide of iron is his own renaming of what chemistry until then had called the rust of iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["Antoine Laurent Lavoisier, <i>Elements of Chemistry, in a New Systematic Order, Containing All the Modern Discoveries</i>, trans. Robert Kerr (Edinburgh: William Creech, 1790), chap. 3, Project Gutenberg, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/30775. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Glass loses quality each time it is recycled, so it can only go round a limited number of times.","a":false,"why":"Glass can be melted and re-formed indefinitely without degrading, which is unusual among common materials; paper fibres shorten and most plastics do lose properties with each cycle. The practical limits on glass recycling are collection and colour sorting rather than the material.","cat":"Chemistry"},
{"q":"Mendeleev left gaps in his periodic table for elements nobody had found, and predicted what they would be like.","a":true,"why":"By 1869 Mendeleev had arranged the known elements by their properties and atomic weights and found prominent gaps, which he named after the element above each one — eka-aluminium, ekasilicon and the rest — and gave a forecast atomic weight and density.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gallium turned up below aluminium in 1875,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> germanium below silicon in 1886, and his ekasilicon weight of 72 against a real 72.6 was the closest of the lot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Chemistry","src":["Royal Society of Chemistry, “Gallium,” Periodic Table, https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/31/gallium. [Open access]","Royal Society of Chemistry, “Germanium,” Periodic Table, https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/32/germanium. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The word 'vaccine' comes from the Latin for cow.","a":true,"why":"Edward Jenner called the cowpox he inoculated with the <i>variolae vaccinae</i> — the smallpox of the cow — and put the phrase in the title of the 1798 <i>Inquiry</i> that reported the procedure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <i>Vaccinus</i> is the Latin adjective from <i>vacca</i>, a cow, and Lewis and Short gloss it simply “of or from cows.”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Edward Jenner, <i>An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolæ Vaccinæ, a Disease Discovered in Some of the Western Counties of England, Particularly Gloucestershire, and Known by the Name of the Cow Pox</i> (London: printed for the author by Sampson Low, 1798), https://archive.org/details/b24759247. [Open access]","Charlton T. Lewis and Charles Short, “vaccīnus,” in <i>A Latin Dictionary</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0059:entry=vaccinus. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Sugar makes children hyperactive.","a":false,"why":"Double-blind trials, in which neither the children nor the adults watching them know whether sugar was given, find no effect on behaviour. In one study parents told their child had been given sugar rated the child as more hyperactive even though the drink was sugar-free.","cat":"Medicine"},
{"q":"Shaving makes hair grow back thicker and darker.","a":false,"why":"A clinical trial as early as 1928 showed that shaving has no effect on hair growth, and later studies confirm it changes neither the thickness nor the rate of regrowth; the razor takes off the dead shaft and leaves the living part below the skin untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It feels coarse because a shaved hair has none of the fine taper an unshaved end has, and looks darker because the new growth has not yet been lightened by the sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Rachel C. Vreeman and Aaron E. Carroll, “Medical myths,” <i>BMJ</i> 335, no. 7633 (2007): 1288–89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2151163/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The active ingredient in aspirin has a natural relative in willow bark, which was used as a medicine for thousands of years before the drug existed.","a":true,"why":"Willow bark has been used medicinally for more than three and a half thousand years: the Sumerians and ancient Egyptians took it as a painkiller and a fever remedy, and Hippocrates gave it for inflammatory pain in the fourth century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Salicin was refined into aspirin in 1897, the name joining acetyl to Spirsäure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Chun-Ru Lin, Sung Huang Laurent Tsai, Che Wang, Cheng-Lin Lee, Shao-Wen Hung, Yi-Tang Ting, and Yu Chiang Hung, “Willow Bark (<i>Salix</i> spp.) Used for Pain Relief in Arthritis: A Meta-Analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials,” <i>Life</i> 13, no. 10 (2023): 2058, https://doi.org/10.3390/life13102058. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Ignaz Semmelweis's evidence that handwashing cut deaths in a maternity ward was accepted by the medical profession as soon as he presented it.","a":false,"why":"In his Vienna clinic 459 of 4,010 women died in 1846, 11.4 per cent; after he required every attendant to wash in chlorine solution from the middle of May 1847 the figure fell to about 5 per cent, and to 1.3 per cent in 1848.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His teachings were, in his own words, either ignored or attacked; he was committed to an asylum in 1865 and died there of sepsis at 47.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Vanessa Ravel, Chandini Pandiyan, and Krupa Chandran, “Ignaz Philipp Semmelweis (1818–1865) – a public health visionary and champion of hand hygiene,” <i>GMS Hygiene and Infection Control</i> 20 (2025): Doc79, https://doi.org/10.3205/dgkh000608. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The summit furthest from the centre of the Earth is not Mount Everest.","a":true,"why":"That distinction belongs to Chimborazo in Ecuador, which stands one degree south of the equator where Earth's bulge is greatest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its summit is 6,268 metres (20,564 feet) above sea level, yet the bulge puts it more than 2,072 metres (6,800 feet) further from Earth's centre than Everest's peak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["National Ocean Service, “What is the highest point on Earth as measured from Earth's center?” NOAA, last updated 16 June 2024, https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/highestpoint.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Sahara has been green grassland with lakes and rivers within the span of human history.","a":true,"why":"A strengthened summer monsoon watered the Sahara, turning the world's largest hot desert into grassland, lakes and rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It began about 15,000 years ago and had closed by roughly 5,500, driven by precession, the slow wobble that brings northern summers nearer the Sun<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> — and Lake Megachad alone covered some 361,000 square kilometres (139,000 square miles), carrying fish and hippopotamus across ground that is now sand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["Martin H. Trauth, Asfawossen Asrat, Nadine Berner, Faysal Bibi, Verena Foerster et al., “Early Warning Signals of the Termination of the African Humid Period(s),” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 3697, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-47921-1. [Open access]","Nick A. Drake, Roger M. Blench, Simon J. Armitage, Charlie S. Bristow, and Kevin H. White, “Ancient Watercourses and Biogeography of the Sahara Explain the Peopling of the Desert,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 2 (2011): 458–62, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1012231108. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Earth's magnetic field has reversed its polarity many times in the geological past.","a":true,"why":"Grains of magnetite in lava line up with the field as the rock cools and hold that direction, and palaeomagnetists found early in the 20th century that rocks fall into two groups, one aligned with the present field and one opposite to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sea floor either side of a mid-ocean ridge carries those two polarities in matching stripes, so the oceanic crust is in effect a tape recording of the reversals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["W. Jacquelyne Kious and Robert I. Tilling, “Developing the Theory,” in <i>This Dynamic Earth: The Story of Plate Tectonics</i> (U.S. Geological Survey, 1996), https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/developing.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Alfred Wegener's proposal that the continents had drifted was accepted by geologists soon after he published it in 1912.","a":false,"why":"The scientific community of 1912 “firmly believed the continents and oceans to be permanent features on the Earth's surface”, and the proposal “was not well received”: its fatal weakness was that it could name no force able to move a continent, and Harold Jeffreys showed that solid rock could not plough through the ocean floor without breaking up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wegener froze to death on the Greenland ice cap in 1930, and it was ocean-floor exploration after his death — seafloor spreading theorised in 1961, and the magnetic striping beside it — that revived the idea and became plate tectonics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["W. Jacquelyne Kious and Robert I. Tilling, “Historical Perspective,” in <i>This Dynamic Earth: The Story of Plate Tectonics</i> (U.S. Geological Survey, 1996), https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/historical.html. [Open access]","W. Jacquelyne Kious and Robert I. Tilling, “Developing the Theory,” in <i>This Dynamic Earth: The Story of Plate Tectonics</i> (U.S. Geological Survey, 1996), https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/developing.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Nothing can travel faster than light in any medium.","a":false,"why":"Nothing outruns light in a vacuum, but light itself slows in matter, and charged particles routinely exceed that reduced speed. The blue glow around a submerged reactor core is Cherenkov radiation, produced by electrons moving through water faster than light does in water.","cat":"Physics"},
{"q":"A hammer and a feather dropped together on the Moon reach the ground at the same moment, and this has been tested there.","a":true,"why":"At the end of the third Apollo 15 moonwalk David Scott released a 1.32-kilogram (2.9-pound) geological hammer and a 0.03-kilogram (1-ounce) falcon feather together from about 1.6 metres (5 feet 3 inches), in front of a live television camera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Preliminary Science Report records that within the accuracy of the release the two underwent the same acceleration and struck the surface at the same moment, there being no air to hold the feather back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Physics","src":["David R. Williams, “The Apollo 15 Hammer-Feather Drop,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, quoting Joe Allen in <i>Apollo 15 Preliminary Science Report</i>, NASA SP-289 (1972), 2-11, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/apollo_15_feather_drop.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The electron was identified before anyone knew that atoms have a nucleus.","a":true,"why":"J. J. Thomson showed in 1897 that cathode rays are made of particles — electrons — that conduct electricity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The nucleus came more than a decade later, out of Rutherford's investigations into the scattering of alpha rays and the inner structure of the atom that caused it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Physics","src":["“J.J. Thomson – Facts,” Nobel Prize Outreach, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/1906/thomson/facts/. [Open access]","“Ernest Rutherford – Biographical,” Nobel Prize Outreach, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/1908/rutherford/biographical/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Absolute zero has been reached in the laboratory.","a":false,"why":"Experiments have come extraordinarily close — within a few hundred trillionths of a degree — but not to zero. The third law of thermodynamics implies it cannot be reached in a finite number of steps, since each stage of cooling removes only a fraction of the remaining energy.","cat":"Physics"},
{"q":"The Moon is slowly moving away from the Earth.","a":true,"why":"NASA’s Moon fact sheet gives a recession rate from the earth of 3.8 centimetres (1.5 inches) a year, against a mean distance of 384,400 kilometres (238,900 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At that rate the moon has moved about 3.8 metres (12 feet) further off since 1926.","cat":"Astronomy","src":["David R. Williams, “Moon Fact Sheet,” NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive, https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/moonfact.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Jupiter is more massive than every other planet in the solar system put together.","a":true,"why":"Jupiter took most of the material left over after the Sun formed, ending up with more than twice the combined mass of everything else orbiting it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is 11 times wider than Earth, and still has nowhere near enough mass to have ignited as a star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["NASA, “Jupiter Facts,” NASA Science, last updated 21 April 2025, https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-facts/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Halley's Comet was first recorded by Edmond Halley.","a":false,"why":"Astronomers have linked the comet's appearances to observations going back more than two thousand years, and it appears in the Bayeux Tapestry's record of the Battle of Hastings in 1066.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Halley's own contribution, in 1705, was to notice that the bright comets of 1531, 1607 and 1682 were one object returning, and to predict correctly that it would come back in 1758.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Astronomy","src":["NASA, “1P/Halley,” NASA Science, last updated 3 November 2024, https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/1p-halley/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Humans are more closely related to mushrooms than mushrooms are to plants.","a":true,"why":"Analysis of the fungal genome shows that fungi, long taken for plants, are more closely related to animals than to plants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They keep other animal habits too: they store carbohydrate as glycogen rather than as starch, and build their cell walls from chitin, the material of an insect's exoskeleton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["OpenStax, “24.1 Characteristics of Fungi,” <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/24-1-characteristics-of-fungi. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A giraffe has the same number of neck vertebrae as a human being.","a":true,"why":"The count of seven cervical vertebrae is highly conserved across mammals, and the giraffe has just seven — long, flexible neck and all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The only mammals that depart from it are the manatees and the two living genera of tree sloth, one running down to five vertebrae and the other up to ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Luisa J. F. Merten, Armita R. Manafzadeh, Eva C. Herbst, Eli Amson, P. Sebastián Tambusso, Patrick Arnold, and John A. Nyakatura, “The functional significance of aberrant cervical counts in sloths: insights from automated exhaustive analysis of cervical range of motion,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 290, no. 2010 (2023): 20231592, https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2023.1592. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The blue whale is the largest living thing that has ever existed on Earth.","a":false,"why":"It is the largest animal, which is the narrower claim. Pando, a single male quaking aspen in Utah whose 40,000-odd trees share one root system, is called by the Forest Service the largest organism ever found, at nearly 5,900 tonnes (13 million pounds) spread over 43 hectares (106 acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is thought to have started at the end of the last ice age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["U.S. Forest Service, “Pando,” Fishlake National Forest, https://www.fs.usda.gov/r04/fishlake/recreation/explore-forest/pando. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Chameleons change colour chiefly to blend in with their surroundings.","a":false,"why":"A comparative study of southern African dwarf chameleons found that the species with the most dramatic colour change are the ones whose social displays contrast most against their background, and found no evidence at all for camouflage driving it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The colours come from actively tuning a lattice of guanine nanocrystals in the skin, over a deeper layer of larger crystals that reflects much of the sunlight falling on the animal in the near infrared and may serve as passive thermal protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Devi Stuart-Fox and Adnan Moussalli, “Selection for Social Signalling Drives the Evolution of Chameleon Colour Change,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 6, no. 1 (2008): e25, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.0060025. [Open access]","Jérémie Teyssier, Suzanne V. Saenko, Dirk van der Marel, and Michel C. Milinkovitch, “Photonic Crystals Cause Active Colour Change in Chameleons,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 6 (2015): 6368, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms7368. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Julius Caesar was assassinated in the Senate House of Rome.","a":false,"why":"The senate was not in the Curia that day: it met in the hall attached to Pompey's theatre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Appian has Caesar turn on one man after another until the blow from Brutus, when he veiled himself and fell at the foot of Pompey's statue — the rival he had beaten — and says several of the assassins wounded each other in the press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 2.117, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/2*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Caesar</i> 66, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 7 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1919), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caesar*.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Marco Polo brought pasta to Italy from China.","a":false,"why":"Pasta is documented in Italy before he returned in 1295 — a Sicilian account of the 1150s describes a dried strand pasta being made near Palermo and exported. The Marco Polo story appears to originate in an American trade magazine article of the 1920s.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},
{"q":"The Wright brothers’ first powered flight in 1903 lasted twelve seconds, and the best flight of that same morning lasted nearly five times as long.","a":true,"why":"Orville flew 36 metres (120 feet) in twelve seconds on the first attempt at Kitty Hawk on 17 December 1903.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wilbur took the controls for the best flight of the day and covered 255.6 metres (852 feet) in 59 seconds, on an aircraft whose own wingspan was 12.3 metres (40 feet 4 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["National Air and Space Museum, “1903 Wright Flyer,” object A19610048000, Smithsonian Institution, https://airandspace.si.edu/collection-objects/1903-wright-flyer/nasm_A19610048000. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The first person to see bacteria did so through lenses he had ground himself.","a":true,"why":"Van Leeuwenhoek's discovery of ‘animalcules’ is where microbiology begins, and the lenses were his own: self-produced, and unsurpassed for more than 150 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neutron tomography inside two of the surviving instruments found the Leiden one's lens ground and polished in the classical way, while the most powerful that survives, at Utrecht, was made by the blown-globule method Robert Hooke had popularised in 1678.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Science & invention","src":["Tiemen Cocquyt, Zhou Zhou, Jeroen Plomp, and Lambert van Eijck, “Neutron Tomography of Van Leeuwenhoek’s Microscopes,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 20 (2021): eabf2402, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abf2402. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The most complete early human skeleton ever found is more than a million years old.","a":true,"why":"The Turkana Boy, the skeleton of a boy who died beside a lake in northern Kenya about 1.6 million years ago, is the most complete early human ever recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “KNM-WT 15000,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/knm-wt-15000. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The smallest braincase found at Dmanisi in Georgia is closer in size to Homo habilis than to Homo erectus.","a":true,"why":"The Dmanisi people were small-bodied, and the smallest of their braincases holds only about 546 cubic centimetres — nearer Homo habilis than the Homo erectus they are usually assigned to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["David Lordkipanidze, Marcia S. Ponce de León, Ann Margvelashvili, et al., “A Complete Skull from Dmanisi, Georgia, and the Evolutionary Biology of Early <i>Homo</i>,” <i>Science</i> 342, no. 6156 (2013): 326–31, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1238484. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"The Acheulean hand-axe tradition begins about 1.4 million years ago.","a":false,"why":"The oldest Acheulean tools come from Kokiselei west of Lake Turkana and from Konso in Ethiopia and are about 1.76 to 1.74 million years old, which pushed the tradition back some 300,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Ignacio de la Torre, “The Origins of the Acheulean: Past and Present Perspectives on a Major Transition in Human Evolution,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150245, under “A Historical Context for the Acheulean” and “The Hard Evidence for the Earliest Acheulean: The Current View,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920301/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Fire became routine human equipment as soon as the first hominins learned to use it.","a":false,"why":"Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa argued in 2011 that a handful of early sites is not the same as habit, and that fire becomes routine equipment only around 400,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa, “On the Earliest Evidence for Habitual Use of Fire in Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 13 (2011): 5209–14, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3069174/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A whole new genus of human was once named on the evidence of a single tooth.","a":true,"why":"In 1927 the Canadian anatomist Davidson Black named the genus and species Sinanthropus pekinensis — Peking Man — on the strength of one lower molar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Song Xing, María Martinón-Torres and José María Bermúdez de Castro, “The Fossil Teeth of the Peking Man,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 2066, introduction, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-20432-y. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Upper Cave at Zhoukoudian, near Beijing, held more remains of Peking Man.","a":false,"why":"Its people were modern humans, at least eight of them, and its cultural layers are dated to roughly 38,300 to 33,500 years ago — hundreds of thousands of years later than the Homo erectus finds at the same site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Francesco d'Errico et al., “Zhoukoudian Upper Cave Personal Ornaments and Ochre: Rediscovery and Reevaluation,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 161 (2021): 103088, §§1–2 and fig. 1, https://shs.hal.science/hal-03445635/document. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The gold-rich Copper Age cemetery at Varna in Bulgaria was in use for well over a thousand years.","a":false,"why":"The Varna necropolis was in use for about 250 years, between 4590 and 4340 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Raiko Krauß et al., “Chronology and Development of the Chalcolithic Necropolis of Varna I,” <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 44 (2017): 282–305, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.44.17. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The oldest known copper smelting comes from the Balkans rather than the Near East.","a":true,"why":"The oldest known copper smelting is slag from Belovode in eastern Serbia, dated to about 5000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Miljana Radivojević and Benjamin W. Roberts, “Early Balkan Metallurgy: Origins, Evolution and Society, 6200–3700 BC,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 34 (2021): 195–278, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-021-09155-7. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"People used copper minerals as ornament long before anyone smelted metal out of them.","a":true,"why":"The first use of copper in the Balkans, from about 6200 BCE, is malachite and azurite ground into beads — minerals picked for their green and blue rather than for any metal in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Miljana Radivojević and Benjamin W. Roberts, “Early Balkan Metallurgy: Origins, Evolution and Society, 6200–3700 BC,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 34 (2021): 195–278, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-021-09155-7. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Yangshao culture of the middle Yellow River flourished roughly between 5000 and 3000 BCE.","a":true,"why":"It is the great Neolithic culture of the middle Yellow River, spread across what are now Henan, Shaanxi and Shanxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Lei Sun et al., “The Demic Expansion of Yangshao Culture Inferred from Ancient Human Genomes,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 23 (2025): 186, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12218936/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"New dating has made the earliest farming settlement at Mehrgarh younger than it was long thought to be.","a":true,"why":"Radiocarbon on tooth enamel from 23 burials puts the aceramic cemetery between 5200 and 4900 BCE, lasting two to five centuries, which overturned its older dates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Benjamin Mutin et al., “New Radiocarbon Dates of Human Tooth Enamel Reveal a Late Appearance of Farming Life in the Indus Valley,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 11345, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-92621-5. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Sahara has been green only once in the recent geological past.","a":false,"why":"More than 230 such green phases have been counted in the last eight million years, paced by the changing shape of the Earth's orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Earth science","src":["Juan C. Larrasoaña, Andrew P. Roberts and Eelco J. Rohling, “Dynamics of Green Sahara Periods and Their Role in Hominin Evolution,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 10 (2013): e76514, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0076514. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"One Saharan massif alone holds more than seven hundred recorded rock-art sites.","a":true,"why":"The Tadrart Acacus in Libya has 707 recorded sites; the great concentrations of Saharan rock art lie in the central massifs, the Acacus and the Tassili n'Ajjer in Algeria above all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Marina Gallinaro, “Saharan Rock Art: Local Dynamics and Wider Perspectives,” <i>Arts</i> 2, no. 4 (2013): 350–82, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts2040350. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Cattle herding reached the Sahara with herders arriving from outside it.","a":false,"why":"By 7,000 years ago people were keeping livestock in the Libyan mountains, and herding seems to have spread among the Saharans already there rather than arriving with newcomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Nada Salem et al., “Ancient DNA from the Green Sahara Reveals Ancestral North African Lineage,” <i>Nature</i> 641, no. 8061 (2025): 144–50, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08793-7. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Jōmon people of Japan are read as descendants of people who reached the islands in the Upper Palaeolithic.","a":true,"why":"They are read as the direct descendants of those who carried Upper Palaeolithic stone tools into the islands some 38,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Takashi Gakuhari et al., “Ancient Jomon Genome Sequence Analysis Sheds Light on Migration Patterns of Early East Asian Populations,” <i>Communications Biology</i> 3 (2020): 437, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-020-01162-2. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Maize was first domesticated in what is now the United States.","a":false,"why":"Maize was made out of teosinte, a Mexican grass, about 9,000 years ago, and reached North America long afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Chin Jian Yang, Luis Fernando Samayoa, Peter J. Bradbury, et al., “The genetic architecture of teosinte catalyzed and constrained maize domestication,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 12 (2019): 5643–5652, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1820997116. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Growing maize, beans and squash together — the Three Sisters — is thousands of years old in the American Midwest and north-east.","a":false,"why":"It became common there only by the middle of the 1500s, so an arrangement most readers take to be ancient is a few generations older than the colonies rather than millennia old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["D. G. Kapayou, E. M. Herrighty, C. Gish Hill, et al., “Reuniting the Three Sisters: collaborative science with Native growers to improve soil and community health,” <i>Agriculture and Human Values</i> 40, no. 1 (2022): 65–82, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9288846/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Four of the five plants first domesticated in eastern North America appear within a narrow window around 3800 years ago.","a":true,"why":"The earliest turn up as charred seeds in rockshelters and river-bank camps in Kentucky, Tennessee, Missouri and Illinois.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Bruce D. Smith and Richard A. Yarnell, “Initial formation of an indigenous crop complex in eastern North America at 3800 B.P.,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 16 (2009): 6561–66, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2666091/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The great earthworks at Poverty Point in Louisiana were raised by farmers.","a":false,"why":"Poverty Point was raised about 3,300 years ago by people who hunted, fished and gathered rather than farmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Seth B. Grooms, Grace M. V. Ward and Tristram R. Kidder, “Convergence at Poverty Point: A Revised Chronology of the Late Archaic Lower Mississippi Valley,” <i>Antiquity</i> 97, no. 396 (2023): 1453–69, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2023.155. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Pottery in Jōmon Japan was used chiefly for storing nuts and seeds.","a":false,"why":"Across 143 vessels spanning nine thousand years, a large share carry the chemical markers of food out of the water, whatever the phase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Alexandre Lucquin et al., “Ancient Lipids Document Continuity in the Use of Early Hunter-Gatherer Pottery through 9,000 Years of Japanese Prehistory,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 15 (2016): 3991–96, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1522908113. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The oldest soybean finds in Japan are older than the oldest azuki bean finds.","a":true,"why":"The oldest Japanese azuki is about 10,000 years old, from the Awazu site in Shiga; the oldest soybean is about 13,000 years old, from Ojiyama in Miyazaki.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Yu Takahashi et al., “Domestication of Azuki Bean and Soybean in Japan: From the Insight of Archeological and Molecular Evidence,” <i>Breeding Science</i> 73, no. 2 (2023): 117–31, https://doi.org/10.1270/jsbbs.22074. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Pottery in Jōmon-period Kyushu was carried across open water between islands.","a":true,"why":"At one site in southern Kyushu about one pot in ten had been carried at least 17 to 33 kilometres (11 to 21 miles), some of it across open channels between islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Prehistory","src":["Fumie Iizuka, Jeffrey R. Ferguson, and Masami Izuho, “Late Pleistocene Pottery Production and Exchange: Provenance Studies of Hunter-Gatherer Wares from Southern Kyushu, Japan by Neutron Activation Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 3 (2022): e0265329, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0265329. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Han River, which runs through Seoul, drains the largest watershed in Korea.","a":true,"why":"Its watershed covers 26,219 square kilometres (10,123 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Jong Kwon Im, Yong Chul Cho, Young Seuk Kim, Soyoung Lee, Taegu Kang and Sang Hun Kim, “Characteristics, Possible Origins, and Health Risk Assessment of Trace Elements in Surface Water of the Han River Watershed,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 23 (2022): 15822, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9741419/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"South Korea has a markedly older population than North Korea.","a":true,"why":"19.3 per cent of the South is 65 or older against 12.4 per cent of the North.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["World Bank, “Population Ages 65 and Above (% of Total Population) — Korea, Rep.; Korea, Dem. People's Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/SP.POP.65UP.TO.ZS?locations=KR-KP. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Korea built a long defensive wall across the northern neck of the peninsula in the 11th century.","a":true,"why":"The Goryeo long wall, finished in 1034, marks where the kingdom's frontier stood against repeated incursions by the Khitan and the Jurchen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Gari Ledyard, “Cartography in Korea,” in <i>The History of Cartography</i>, vol. 2, book 2, ed. J. B. Harley and David Woodward (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1994), 235–344, https://press.uchicago.edu/books/HOC/HOC_V2_B2/HOC_VOLUME2_Book2_chapter10.pdf. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Human sacrifice in China begins with the Shang dynasty.","a":false,"why":"The practice is older: at Shimao, a walled city on the Loess Plateau, more than 80 human skulls lie buried beneath the East Gate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Zehui Chen et al., “Ancient DNA from Shimao City Records Kinship Practices in Neolithic China,” <i>Nature</i> 648, no. 8094 (2025): 659–67, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12711557/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The traditional list of Shang kings runs to thirty-one.","a":true,"why":"Wu Ding stands 22nd in that list and Di Xin last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The tomb of Fu Hao held about 1,500 objects.","a":true,"why":"Among them were weapons, bells, blades, mirrors and bronze sculptures of tigers, together with a great quantity of jade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"By the late phases at Anyang, cattle had passed pigs in dietary importance even in ordinary quarters.","a":true,"why":"One head of cattle yields over 200 kilograms (440 pounds) of meat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The tallest and heaviest bronze the Shang ever cast is over a metre high.","a":true,"why":"It stands 133 centimetres (52 inches) high and came out of tomb M260 at Xibeigang, east of the cemetery near Wuguancun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Chinese history","src":["Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Mycenae was still a functioning town when Athens and Sparta went to war.","a":false,"why":"In 468 BCE the Argives besieged and stormed it, sold its people into slavery, dedicated a tenth of them to the god and razed the town — though the walls were beyond removing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.65, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0084%3Abook%3D11%3Achapter%3D65. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Mycenae has a single tholos tomb, the so-called Treasury of Atreus.","a":false,"why":"Nine of them stand at Mycenae, built between about 1525 and 1275 BCE, and Alan Wace sorted them into three groups by their masonry and their ornament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Skulls from a royal grave circle at Mycenae have been rebuilt as faces.","a":true,"why":"Seven skulls from Grave Circle B were rebuilt in 1995, and the reconstruction found the man in the electrum mask to resemble the woman buried beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["J. H. Musgrave, R. A. H. Neave and A. J. N. W. Prag, \"Seven Faces from Grave Circle B at Mycenae,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 90 (1995): 107–36, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0068245400016105. [Paywalled]"]},
{"q":"Cumae, on the Bay of Naples, is the oldest Greek city on the Italian mainland.","a":true,"why":"It was in the ground no later than 730 to 720 BCE and was settled from Pithekoussai, the island off the bay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The Capitoline is the largest of Rome's seven hills.","a":false,"why":"It is the smallest, some 460 metres (500 yards) long and 180 metres (200 yards) wide on average, lying between the Forum and the Campus Martius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Capitolinus Mons,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 95–98, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Capitolinus.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A hut on the Palatine said to be Romulus's own was still on record a thousand years after Rome was founded.","a":true,"why":"The casa Romuli was still recorded in the 4th century CE, more than a thousand years after the settlement it stood for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Ancient & classical","src":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Casa Romuli,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 101–2, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Casa_Romuli.html. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The League of Nations began with fewer than a dozen member states.","a":false,"why":"It came into existence on 10 January 1920, and forty-one states opened its first Assembly at Geneva that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["United Nations Office at Geneva, \"The League of Nations,\" UN Library and Archives Geneva, https://www.ungeneva.org/en/library-archives/league-of-nations. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Every treaty that ended the First World War was imposed on the losing side rather than negotiated.","a":false,"why":"The Treaty of Lausanne of 24 July 1923, between Turkey and the Allied powers, was the one post-war settlement that was genuinely negotiated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Modern history","src":["Alan Sharp, \"The Paris Peace Conference and its Consequences,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2022), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/the-paris-peace-conference-and-its-consequences-1-1/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"Industrially produced trans fat is estimated to cause up to half a million deaths a year.","a":true,"why":"It is a by-product of hardening vegetable oils, and it is entirely replaceable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Medicine","src":["Lindsay Steele, Elizabeth Drummond, Chizuru Nishida, Rain Yamamoto, Francesco Branca, Cristina Parsons Perez, Lorena Allemandi, et al., “Ending Trans Fat: The First-Ever Global Elimination Program for a Noncommunicable Disease Risk Factor,” <i>Journal of the American College of Cardiology</i> 84, no. 7 (2024): 663–74, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2024.04.067. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"The alpha helix and the beta sheet were first described after the structure of DNA was solved in 1953.","a":false,"why":"Both were proposed by Linus Pauling and Robert Corey in 1951, two years before the DNA double helix.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A protein cannot find its folded shape by trying conformations at random.","a":true,"why":"Levinthal's paradox of 1969 pointed out that sampling them at random is implausible — there are far too many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Omer Faruk Kuzu, Lars Jørgen Tvenge Granerud, and Fahri Saatcioglu, “Navigating the Landscape of Protein Folding and Proteostasis: From Molecular Chaperones to Therapeutic Innovations,” <i>Signal Transduction and Targeted Therapy</i> 10, no. 1 (2025): 358, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41392-025-02439-w. [Open access]"]},
{"q":"A fatty acid written as 18:1 has eighteen carbon atoms and one double bond.","a":true,"why":"Chains are written as a pair of numbers giving length and the count of double bonds, so palmitate is 16:0, stearate 18:0 and oleate 18:1.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","cat":"Biology","src":["Jacob T. Talley and Shamim S. Mohiuddin, “Biochemistry, Fatty Acid Oxidation,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556002/. [Open access]"]}
];
